const e={intro:["A council is the church arguing in public and then writing down what it decided. Nothing else in Christian history is quite like it. The scriptures are received rather than voted; the creeds are the councils' residue; the theologians may be brilliant and still be wrong. A council is a room full of bishops — some frightened, some bought, a few of them saints — trying to say in Greek what had been said in Aramaic by a carpenter's son, and to say it so that the next generation could not mistake it. That they largely succeeded is among the stranger facts in the history of ideas. That they also broke the church doing it is the second.","The count is the map. Ask a Christian how many ecumenical councils there have been and the answer tells you which church he belongs to and which schism made it. The Assyrian Church of the East counts two, stopping at Constantinople in 381. The Oriental Orthodox — Copts, Syriacs, Armenians, Ethiopians, Eritreans, the Malankara Orthodox of India — count three, stopping at Ephesus in 431. The Eastern Orthodox count seven, stopping at Nicaea in 787. The Catholic Church counts twenty-one and is still counting. Most Protestants receive the doctrine of the first four without granting councils the authority to bind, the Anglican Articles putting it bluntly that general councils may err and sometimes have erred. Two, three, seven, twenty-one: every number is a wound, and the wound is dated.","The councils are not, however, where most of the work happened. A council ratifies; it rarely thinks. The thinking happens in the decades of quarrel that precede it and the centuries of quarrel that follow, in letters and pamphlets and provincial synods and prison cells — in the arguments. Half of this page is therefore given to those: the disputes that ran between the councils and, in several cases, ran straight through them and out the other side, unfinished.","Every party here is described as it would describe itself. Heresiarchs are given the courtesy of an accurate account of what they actually taught, which is nearly always more interesting and more defensible than the caricature; their opponents are given the courtesy of an accurate account of why they objected, which is nearly always more serious than mere power. Where a church rejects the name its enemies gave it, this page uses the name it gives itself. Where the tradition's memory and the historians' reconstruction diverge, both are set down and attributed. This is living faith for well over two billion people, in communions that once anathematised one another and are now, slowly and incompletely, learning to read one another's words."],argumentsIntro:"Councils are punctuation. The sentences are written in between — in letters carried by ship, in pamphlets composed in exile, in provincial synods that nobody remembers, in the long arguments that a council may close on paper without closing at all. Several of the quarrels below were never settled and are still live: a Reformed congregation stripping its walls is still answering Nicaea II, an analytic philosopher defending middle knowledge is still answering Gottschalk, and a Greek and a Latin discussing the procession of the Spirit are still, after twelve hundred years, sorting out whether they disagree. What follows is the argument itself, at the length the argument needs, with the losing case put as its holders put it.",councils:[{slug:"jerusalem-49",name:"The Council at Jerusalem",year:49,place:"Jerusalem",convokedBy:"The apostles and elders of the Jerusalem church, in answer to a delegation from Antioch led by Paul and Barnabas",question:"Must gentiles who turn to Christ be circumcised and keep the law of Moses in order to be saved?",decision:"No. Peter argued from his own experience at the house of Cornelius that God had given the Spirit to the uncircumcised and had made no distinction; Paul and Barnabas reported what had happened among the gentiles; James, presiding, gave sentence from Amos 9 that the nations were always to be gathered in, and ruled that the church should not trouble those of the gentiles who turn to God. Four abstentions were nevertheless enjoined, in a letter carried to Antioch by Judas Barsabbas and Silas: from what has been polluted by idols, from sexual immorality, from what is strangled, and from blood. The letter's preamble — that it seemed good to the Holy Spirit and to us — became the grammar of every council afterwards. The date is inferred from Pauline chronology rather than given by any source, and c. 48–50 is the usual range.",keyTerms:[{term:"edoxen to pneumati to hagio kai hemin",native:"ἔδοξεν τῷ πνεύματι τῷ ἁγίῳ καὶ ἡμῖν",meaning:"'It seemed good to the Holy Spirit and to us' (Acts 15:28): the formula by which a human assembly claims to speak with God's voice without claiming to be God. Every conciliar decree since is a variation on it."},{term:"eidolothyta",native:"εἰδωλόθυτα",meaning:"Food sacrificed to idols — the first of the four abstentions, and the one Paul would treat far more flexibly in 1 Corinthians 8-10, which is itself evidence of how the decree was received."},{term:"pnikton",native:"πνικτόν",meaning:"What is strangled, i.e. meat from which the blood has not been drained. With blood itself, this is the piece of the decree that Western Christianity quietly dropped and the Eastern and Ethiopian traditions did not."},{term:"porneia",native:"πορνεία",meaning:"Sexual immorality: whether this means pagan sexual licence generally or specifically the forbidden degrees of kinship in Leviticus 18 — which would make all four items a list of what Leviticus requires of the resident alien — is disputed among exegetes."}],dissent:"Some believers who belonged to the party of the Pharisees held that gentiles must be circumcised and charged to keep the law (Acts 15:5), and there is no sign the ruling persuaded them. Whether it settled anything at all is doubtful: the confrontation at Antioch in Galatians 2, where Paul opposes Peter to his face over table-fellowship, reads like a dispute that the decree ought to have prevented. Whether Galatians 2 records this same meeting or an earlier one is one of the durable cruxes of New Testament chronology, and the two accounts differ in ways — the presence or absence of the decree above all — that scholars have never harmonised to general satisfaction. No church today descends from the circumcision party, though Jewish-Christian communities such as the Ebionites and Nazoraeans persisted for centuries, known to us almost entirely through hostile witnesses.",aftermath:"The apostolic decree had a long afterlife: the prohibition of blood is renewed in the Apostolic Canons and again in canon 67 of the Council in Trullo, is still observed by the Ethiopian Orthodox Tewahedo Church and by many Orthodox, and lies behind the refusal of blood transfusion by Jehovah's Witnesses, who read it as a standing divine law. More importantly, Jerusalem set the shape of everything after it — a disputed question, an assembly, testimony, scripture, a ruling, a letter — and set the precedent that a genuinely new thing may be recognised as continuous with the old. Every later council is an appeal to this one.",receivedBy:"By everyone, and by nobody as a council. No communion numbers it in its ecumenical list, because it is not a council of the church but a chapter of scripture; it is received as Acts is received. It is nonetheless the prototype to which all four counting traditions appeal, and the only assembly in this list that Baptists and Copts and Assyrians will all cite as normative.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Acts 15:1–35; Galatians 2:1–14",note:"The two accounts whose relation is the crux — read together, they are the evidence that the assembly did not settle what it is said to have settled."},{kind:"lexicon",ref:"Bruce M. Metzger, A Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament",note:"Where the variant forms of the apostolic decree are set out, including the Western text that drops 'what is strangled' and adds the golden rule."},{kind:"study",ref:"C. K. Barrett, A Critical and Exegetical Commentary on the Acts of the Apostles (ICC), 2 vols",note:"The fullest English commentary on the chapter; cautious about how much of the speeches is Luke's composition."},{kind:"study",ref:"Joseph A. Fitzmyer, The Acts of the Apostles (Anchor Bible)",note:"Lays out the chronological options for Galatians 2 and the Levitical reading of the four abstentions without pretending either is settled."},{kind:"primary",ref:"The Apostolic Canons; Council in Trullo, canon 67 — both in Henry R. Percival, The Seven Ecumenical Councils (NPNF, second series, vol. 14)",note:"The two canonical renewals of the prohibition of blood, which is how the decree stayed law in the East after the West let it go."}]},{slug:"nicaea-i-325",name:"The First Council of Nicaea",year:325,place:"Nicaea in Bithynia (İznik)",convokedBy:"The Emperor Constantine, in the first year in which such a thing was possible, presiding in person over bishops who bore the scars of persecution",question:"Is the Son of God a creature — however exalted, however prior to all other creatures — or is he of the very substance of the Father?",decision:"That he is of the very substance of the Father. The creed confesses the Son as begotten of the Father, only-begotten, that is from the substance of the Father, God from God, light from light, true God from true God, begotten not made, of one substance with the Father. A single closing anathema follows, against those who say that there was when he was not, that before being begotten he was not, that he came to be out of nothing, that he is of another hypostasis or substance, or that he is created, mutable or alterable. The council also fixed the reckoning of Easter, ruled on the Melitian schism in Egypt, and issued twenty canons. Tradition numbers the fathers at three hundred and eighteen, the number of Abraham's trained servants in Genesis 14; contemporary estimates run between two hundred and fifty and three hundred.",keyTerms:[{term:"homoousios",native:"ὁμοούσιος",meaning:"Of one substance, consubstantial. The word is not in scripture; it had been used in Valentinian sources; it had been rejected at Antioch in 268 in connection with Paul of Samosata; and it could be read to mean that Father and Son are merely one thing under two names. That it was chosen anyway, precisely because no follower of Arius could sign it, is the council's decisive act and the source of sixty years of subsequent trouble."},{term:"ek tes ousias tou patros",native:"ἐκ τῆς οὐσίας τοῦ πατρός",meaning:"'From the substance of the Father' — the phrase that rules out the Son's deriving from the divine will, as a creature does, rather than from the divine being."},{term:"gennethenta ou poiethenta",native:"γεννηθέντα οὐ ποιηθέντα",meaning:"'Begotten, not made': the distinction on which the whole argument turns. Begetting yields the same nature; making yields another."},{term:"en pote hote ouk en",native:"ἦν ποτε ὅτε οὐκ ἦν",meaning:"'There was when he was not' — Arius's own formula, anathematised in the creed's closing clause. Note its precision: the word is ποτε, 'once', not χρόνος, 'time', since on Arius's own account time itself was made through the Son."}],dissent:"Arius and two bishops, Theonas of Marmarica and Secundus of Ptolemais, refused to subscribe and were exiled; Eusebius of Nicomedia and Theognis of Nicaea signed the creed but balked at the anathema and were exiled shortly after — and were back within a few years, which is the real story of the next half-century. Arius himself taught that the Son is the first and greatest of all that the Father made, made before all ages and the agent of every other making, truly Son by grace and adoption and not by nature, and that the Father alone is unoriginate: a doctrine advanced in defence of the Father's absolute uniqueness, not in disparagement of Christ, and one that could quote scripture at length. No church descends from Arius; his faith was carried to the Goths by Ulfilas and ruled the Germanic kingdoms for three centuries before dying out. Several living communions do not confess the Nicene faith and should not be confused with him. Jehovah's Witnesses hold that Christ is God's first creation and reject the Trinity on scriptural grounds; The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints confesses the Father, Son and Holy Ghost as three distinct personages one in purpose, and understands itself as restored rather than reformed; Oneness Pentecostals confess the full deity of Christ but as the one God manifest, not as a distinct person, and baptise in Jesus' name; the Unitarian tradition — historically a Christian confession of the unity of God, still held in that form by the Hungarian and Transylvanian Unitarian churches and by British Unitarian congregations, while Unitarian Universalism in North America is today a largely non-creedal association requiring no christology of its members. Each of these arrived at its position from scripture and its own history, and none claims descent from Arius.",aftermath:"Nicaea did not end the controversy; it began it. The sixty years between this council and 381 are the most confused in Christian doctrinal history, with perhaps a dozen rival creeds, Athanasius exiled five times, and a period in which the imperial church was officially Homoian — which drew from Jerome the line that the whole world groaned and marvelled to find itself Arian. The word ὁμοούσιος had to be rescued from its own ambiguity by the Cappadocians before it could carry the weight placed on it.",receivedBy:"By all four counting traditions and by nearly all Protestants: the first council for the Church of the East, for the Oriental Orthodox, for the Eastern Orthodox and for the Catholic Church alike. Among the historic communions it is the one point in this list at which the map shows no fracture; the bodies that do not confess its creed, named above, are later and stand outside that map rather than dividing it.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Norman P. Tanner (ed.), Decrees of the Ecumenical Councils, vol. 1 (1990)",note:"The standard English of the creed, the closing anathema and the twenty canons, with Greek and Latin facing."},{kind:"primary",ref:"H.-G. Opitz (ed.), Urkunden zur Geschichte des arianischen Streites (Athanasius Werke III/1)",note:"The dossier of letters — Arius's own included — from which any account of what was actually at issue has to start."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Athanasius, De decretis Nicaenae synodi; Eusebius of Caesarea's letter to his church at Caesarea",note:"The two ancient defences of ὁμοούσιος, one by a partisan and one by a man explaining to his flock why he had signed."},{kind:"lexicon",ref:"J. N. D. Kelly, Early Christian Creeds, 3rd ed. (1972)",note:"The standard account of the creed's text and of the Antioch 268 difficulty over the word."},{kind:"study",ref:"R. P. C. Hanson, The Search for the Christian Doctrine of God: The Arian Controversy 318–381 (Edinburgh, 1988)",note:"The book that established that the sixty years after the council, not the council itself, are where the doctrine was decided."},{kind:"study",ref:"Rowan Williams, Arius: Heresy and Tradition (rev. ed.)",note:"Reconstructs what Arius taught from his own surviving words rather than from his opponents' summaries."}]},{slug:"constantinople-i-381",name:"The First Council of Constantinople",year:381,place:"Constantinople",convokedBy:"The Emperor Theodosius I, summoning the Eastern bishops only — about a hundred and fifty of them, with no Western delegates and no legates from Rome",question:"Is the Holy Spirit God, as the Son is God — and if so, why does scripture never quite say so?",decision:"That the Spirit is to be worshipped and glorified with the Father and the Son. The creed we call Niceno-Constantinopolitan confesses the Holy Spirit as the Lord, the giver of life, who proceeds from the Father, who with the Father and the Son together is worshipped and glorified, who spoke by the prophets. The economy of that phrasing is deliberate: the council declined to call the Spirit ὁμοούσιος or, in so many words, God, preferring to say what the liturgy already did — that the Spirit is worshipped — and let the inference stand. The first canon anathematises Eunomians, Arians, Pneumatomachi, Sabellians, Marcellians, Photinians and Apollinarians together; the third gives Constantinople the primacy of honour after Rome, because it is New Rome.",keyTerms:[{term:"to ek tou patros ekporeuomenon",native:"τὸ ἐκ τοῦ πατρὸς ἐκπορευόμενον",meaning:"'Who proceeds from the Father': four words that would, seven centuries later, divide Christendom, when the Latin West added 'and the Son' to them."},{term:"to kyrion kai to zoopoion",native:"τὸ κύριον καὶ τὸ ζωοποιόν",meaning:"'The Lord and the giver of life' — attributing to the Spirit both the divine title and the divine work, without the contested technical term."},{term:"pneumatomachoi",native:"πνευματομάχοι",meaning:"'Spirit-fighters'. The name is their opponents', not their own; those it describes, associated with Macedonius of Constantinople, confessed the Son's full divinity and held the Spirit to be a ministering creature. Their case was that scripture nowhere calls the Spirit God and that the church should not go beyond it."},{term:"mia ousia, treis hypostaseis",native:"οὐσία / ὑπόστασις",meaning:"One substance, three hypostases: the Cappadocian settlement that made the doctrine speakable, distinguishing what is common in God from what is proper to each. At Nicaea the two words had been used as synonyms; here they are prised apart, and the Latin persona could at last be matched to ὑπόστασις rather than to οὐσία."}],dissent:"Thirty-six bishops of that party, under Eleusius of Cyzicus and Marcian of Lampsacus, withdrew rather than subscribe, and the party did not survive the century. Nobody exists today because of this refusal. What does survive is the third canon: Rome never accepted Constantinople's ranking, and the principle at stake — that ecclesiastical rank follows political importance rather than apostolic foundation — is one of the seeds of the East-West separation, replanted at Chalcedon and never uprooted.",aftermath:"Historians note that the creed attributed to this council is first quoted as its work at Chalcedon seventy years later, and the relation between the text of 381 and the text of 325 has been debated since Hort — whether the council edited Nicaea, adopted an existing baptismal creed of Jerusalem or Antioch, or produced nothing of the sort and had the creed attached to it retrospectively. J. N. D. Kelly's reconstruction remains the standard account, and it is a reconstruction. What is certain is that this is the creed the churches actually sing: at every Orthodox and Catholic liturgy, in the Anglican and Lutheran rites, in Armenian and Coptic and Syriac, the words of 381 are the ones the congregation knows by heart.",receivedBy:"By all four traditions. It is the second and final ecumenical council for the Assyrian Church of the East, whose reckoning stops here; the second of three for the Oriental Orthodox; the second of seven for the Eastern Orthodox; the second of twenty-one for the Catholic Church. It became ecumenical retroactively, being at the time a council of the East alone: Rome received its creed long before it acknowledged its status, and never received its third canon at all.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Norman P. Tanner (ed.), Decrees of the Ecumenical Councils, vol. 1 (1990)",note:"The creed as Chalcedon quotes it, together with the four canons — including the third, which Rome never received."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Gregory of Nazianzus, De vita sua (Carmina II.1.11)",note:"An eyewitness account by the man who presided and then resigned, and it is not a flattering one."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Basil of Caesarea, On the Holy Spirit; Gregory of Nazianzus, Oration 31",note:"The argument for the Spirit's divinity made first by liturgical reticence and then, in Gregory, said outright."},{kind:"lexicon",ref:"J. N. D. Kelly, Early Christian Creeds, 3rd ed. (1972)",note:"The reconstruction of how the text of 381 relates to that of 325 — the standard account, and, as the entry says, a reconstruction."},{kind:"study",ref:"F. J. A. Hort, Two Dissertations (Cambridge, 1876)",note:"Where the modern doubt about the creed's attribution to this council begins."},{kind:"study",ref:"Lewis Ayres, Nicaea and its Legacy (Oxford, 2004)",note:"Sets 381 as the close of a long terminological settlement rather than as a fresh definition."}]},{slug:"ephesus-431",name:"The Council of Ephesus",year:431,place:"Ephesus, in the church of Mary",convokedBy:"The Emperor Theodosius II; opened by Cyril of Alexandria before the Syrian bishops or the Roman legates had arrived",question:"May Mary be called θεοτόκος, God-bearer — and behind that, is the union in Christ a union of person, or a conjunction of two who remain two?",decision:"That she may, and that the union is one of person. The council approved Cyril's second letter to Nestorius, deposed Nestorius, and declared the Nicene faith sufficient — its seventh canon forbidding anyone to compose or propose another creed, a canon later turned against the Latin West over the filioque. Cyril's Twelve Anathemas were read into the record, but whether the council formally ratified them as its own is disputed; on the usual reckoning they acquired conciliar authority only at Constantinople in 553, whose own anathemas presuppose them. Four days later John of Antioch arrived with the Syrians, held a counter-council, and deposed Cyril; the emperor at first confirmed both depositions. The genuine settlement came two years afterwards in the Formula of Reunion of 433, drafted probably by Theodoret and accepted by Cyril in the letter beginning Laetentur caeli — a text that confesses θεοτόκος and a union of two natures in the same breath, and which is, in substance, what Chalcedon would say.",keyTerms:[{term:"theotokos",native:"θεοτόκος",meaning:"God-bearer, Mother of God. The word is Marian in form and christological in function: to say it is to say that the one born of Mary is, without qualification, the eternal Son."},{term:"christotokos",native:"χριστοτόκος",meaning:"Christ-bearer. Nestorius proposed it as a via media, and also accepted θεοτόκος when properly explained; his objection was that θεοτόκος alone, unglossed, could be heard to say that the Godhead had a beginning or that the divine nature suffered."},{term:"henosis kath' hypostasin",native:"ἕνωσις καθ᾽ ὑπόστασιν",meaning:"Hypostatic union — Cyril's term: the natures are united not by association or indwelling but at the level of the one subject who is Christ, so that what is said of the man may be said of God and conversely."},{term:"qnoma",native:"ܩܢܘܡܐ",meaning:"The Syriac term, in the christology of the Church of the East, for the individual concrete subsistence of a nature — neither the Greek ὑπόστασις nor οὐσία exactly. Their confession of two kyane (ܟܝܢܐ, natures) and two qnome in one parsopa (ܦܪܨܘܦܐ) of union is untranslatable into the Greek debate without distortion, which is a large part of why the separation happened."}],dissent:"The Assyrian Church of the East, which venerates Nestorius together with Diodore of Tarsus and Theodore of Mopsuestia, and which rejects the label Nestorian: it is a name given by others, and the church names itself the Holy Apostolic Catholic Assyrian Church of the East. Its christology is its own, not a borrowing from Nestorius, and its formula of two kyane and two qnome in one parsopa is intended to secure precisely what Chalcedon intended — that the humanity is real and entire and that Christ is one. Nestorius himself taught that in Christ there are two complete natures, each with its own concrete reality, conjoined in one πρόσωπον of union, so that the Word is not born, does not suffer and does not die in his own nature; his fear was of a God who is changed by becoming flesh. What his opponents heard in it was two sons, and a salvation that never reached the human being it was meant to save. The Common Christological Declaration signed by Pope John Paul II and Patriarch Mar Dinkha IV on 11 November 1994 states a shared faith in the one Christ, true God and true man, and recognises both 'Mother of Christ our God and Saviour' and 'Mother of God' as legitimate expressions of it.",aftermath:"Nestorius was exiled to the Great Oasis in Egypt. His apology, the Book of Heracleides, was lost for some fourteen hundred years; a Syriac version was identified in 1889 and published by Bedjan in 1910, and in it Nestorius welcomes the faith of Leo's Tome as the faith for which he had himself been condemned. Since its recovery the scholarly assessment of what he actually held has been contested and revised — a reminder that the church condemned a set of propositions, and the question of whether the man held them is a separate one. The Antiochene tradition he stood in migrated east of the Roman frontier, made Seleucia-Ctesiphon its centre and Persian rather than Roman law its condition, and carried the gospel to India, Central Asia and Tang China.",receivedBy:"By the Oriental Orthodox as their third and last ecumenical council; by the Eastern Orthodox as their third of seven; by the Catholic Church as its third of twenty-one. Not by the Church of the East, whose reckoning ends at 381. Most Protestants receive its christological substance while numbering no councils.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Eduard Schwartz (ed.), Acta Conciliorum Oecumenicorum I (Berlin, 1927–30)",note:"The critical edition of the acts, including the rival Antiochene proceedings that the Greek conciliar collections preserve alongside Cyril's."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Richard Price and Thomas Graumann, The Council of Ephesus of 431: Documents and Proceedings (Translated Texts for Historians, Liverpool)",note:"The acts in English, with an introduction that addresses directly whether the Twelve Anathemas were ever formally ratified."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Cyril of Alexandria, Second and Third Letters to Nestorius, and Laetentur caeli — in Lionel R. Wickham, Cyril of Alexandria: Select Letters (Oxford Early Christian Texts, 1983)",note:"The letter the council approved, the letter carrying the anathemas whose status is disputed, and the letter of 433 in which Cyril accepts two-nature language."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Nestorius, The Bazaar of Heracleides, trans. G. R. Driver and Leonard Hodgson (Oxford, 1925); Syriac ed. Paul Bedjan (Paris, 1910)",note:"The apology recovered after fourteen centuries, in which he welcomes Leo's Tome — the text that reopened the question of what he held."},{kind:"study",ref:"John A. McGuckin, St Cyril of Alexandria: The Christological Controversy (Leiden, 1994)",note:"The fullest account of the council's procedure, and unsentimental about how Cyril managed it."},{kind:"study",ref:"Sebastian P. Brock, 'The Nestorian Church: A Lamentable Misnomer,' Bulletin of the John Rylands Library 78 (1996)",note:"Why the Church of the East's two qnome in one parsopa is not Nestorius's christology and why the label has stuck anyway."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Common Christological Declaration between the Catholic Church and the Assyrian Church of the East (11 November 1994)",note:"The text itself, for exactly what was and was not agreed about θεοτόκος."}]},{slug:"ephesus-ii-449",name:"The Second Council of Ephesus, which Leo called the latrocinium",year:449,place:"Ephesus",convokedBy:"The Emperor Theodosius II, at the urging of Dioscorus of Alexandria, who presided",question:"Was the archimandrite Eutyches rightly deposed at Constantinople the year before for refusing to say that Christ is of two natures after the union?",decision:"That he was not. The council rehabilitated Eutyches, deposed Flavian of Constantinople who had condemned him, and deposed also Ibas of Edessa, Theodoret of Cyrrhus and Domnus of Antioch. Leo's Tome, sent from Rome to be read, was not read; the legates protested and one of them, the deacon Hilarus — later himself pope — escaped with a single word of dissent, contradicitur, and got home to tell it. Flavian died within days, on the road to exile; the Chalcedonian sources say from a beating received in the church, and historians treat the manner of his death as reported by hostile witnesses and uncertain.",keyTerms:[{term:"latrocinium",native:null,meaning:"'Banditry', a den of robbers, whence the English 'Robber Council'. Leo the Great's word, from his letter to the Empress Pulcheria of 20 July 451: not a judgement but a latrocinium. It is a polemical name coined by the losing side's chief advocate two years after the event, and it has become the standard name in Western textbooks — a fact worth pausing over. The Oriental Orthodox use neither it nor its English form, and call the assembly simply the Second Council of Ephesus."},{term:"homoousios hemin",native:"ὁμοούσιος ἡμῖν",meaning:"'Consubstantial with us' — the phrase Eutyches would not clearly affirm of Christ's humanity, and the precise point on which his own tradition, as well as his accusers, judged him wrong."},{term:"contradicitur",native:null,meaning:"'It is contradicted' — the Latin legate's formal protest, recorded in the acts, and effectively the whole of Rome's participation in the council."}],dissent:"Effectively everyone, in the end. Two years later Chalcedon annulled its acts, restored those it had deposed, and deposed Dioscorus in his turn — though on charges of contumacy and misconduct rather than heresy, a distinction the Oriental Orthodox insist upon. No communion numbers 449 among its ecumenical councils, the Oriental Orthodox included, whose list is Nicaea, Constantinople and Ephesus 431. But the Coptic Orthodox Church honours Dioscorus as a saint and confessor unjustly deposed and exiled, and rejects both the polemical name and the account of his conduct that comes to us through Chalcedonian acts. Eutyches, meanwhile, is condemned by the Oriental Orthodox as firmly as by Chalcedon: Severus of Antioch anathematised him, and the miaphysite tradition has always held that a Christ whose humanity is absorbed into his divinity is no saviour of humanity at all.",aftermath:"What Eutyches actually taught is worth stating plainly, since he is the hinge on which the vocabulary turns. He held that Christ was of two natures before the union and one nature after it; pressed at Constantinople in 448, he would not say that the flesh of Christ is consubstantial with ours, and he seems to have thought the humanity swallowed in the divinity as a drop of honey in the sea — an image his opponents attributed to him rather than one preserved in his own words. This is monophysitism properly so called, and it is a heresy in every ancient communion. The council collapsed with its emperor: Theodosius II fell from his horse and died in July 450, his sister Pulcheria and her husband Marcian reversed the policy, and Chalcedon followed within the year.",receivedBy:"By no communion as ecumenical. It is the one council in this list that the whole family agrees was not one — while disagreeing sharply about why, about the justice done to Dioscorus, and about the name it should be given.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Johannes Flemming (ed.), Akten der ephesinischen Synode vom Jahre 449 (Berlin, 1917); English in S. G. F. Perry, The Second Synod of Ephesus (1881)",note:"The Syriac acts of the second session — the only substantial record of the council that does not reach us through Chalcedon's reading of it."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Leo the Great, Ep. 95, to the Empress Pulcheria",note:"Where the word latrocinium is coined, two years after the event and by the losing side's chief advocate."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Richard Price and Michael Gaddis, The Acts of the Council of Chalcedon, 3 vols (Translated Texts for Historians, Liverpool, 2005)",note:"Session I reads the 448 and 449 proceedings back into the record, which is how most of 449 survives — through the court that annulled it."},{kind:"study",ref:"Fergus Millar, A Greek Roman Empire: Power and Belief under Theodosius II (Berkeley, 2006)",note:"The imperial machinery behind both 449 and its reversal, treated as government rather than as theology."},{kind:"study",ref:"Aloys Grillmeier, Christ in Christian Tradition, vol. 1",note:"For what Eutyches can be shown to have said as distinct from what was attributed to him — the honey-in-the-sea image included."}]},{slug:"chalcedon-451",name:"The Council of Chalcedon",year:451,place:"Chalcedon in Bithynia (Kadıköy), in the martyrium of St Euphemia",convokedBy:"The Emperor Marcian and the Empress Pulcheria; the largest assembly of the ancient church, traditionally six hundred and thirty bishops, in fact perhaps five hundred and twenty",question:"Is the one Christ to be acknowledged in two natures, or out of two natures which are thereafter one?",decision:"In two. The Definition confesses one and the same Christ, Son, Lord, only-begotten, acknowledged in two natures without confusion, without change, without division, without separation — the difference of the natures being in no way removed by the union, but rather the property of each preserved and concurring into one person and one hypostasis. Mary is θεοτόκος according to the manhood. Leo's Tome was read and acclaimed, in the words the acts record: Peter has spoken thus through Leo. The Nicene and Constantinopolitan creeds were confirmed, Ephesus 449 annulled, Dioscorus deposed, and twenty-eight canons issued — the last of which, granting Constantinople equal privileges with Old Rome, Leo refused to confirm.",keyTerms:[{term:"en duo physesin",native:"ἐν δύο φύσεσιν",meaning:"'In two natures'. The Greek manuscript tradition preserves both this and ἐκ δύο φύσεων, 'out of two natures', and the acts show the council debating precisely which preposition to use. One preposition separates two communions to this day."},{term:"asynchytos, atreptos, adiairetos, achoristos",native:"ἀσυγχύτως, ἀτρέπτως, ἀδιαιρέτως, ἀχωρίστως",meaning:"Without confusion, without change, without division, without separation. Four adverbs, all negative: the definition says what the union is not, and declines to say what it is. This apophatic reticence is the Definition's genius and, to its critics, its evasion."},{term:"prosopon kai hypostasis",native:"πρόσωπον / ὑπόστασις",meaning:"Person and hypostasis, here treated as equivalent and set against φύσις, nature. Christ is one who, in two whats."},{term:"mia physis tou theou logou sesarkomene",native:"μία φύσις τοῦ θεοῦ λόγου σεσαρκωμένη",meaning:"'One incarnate nature of God the Word' — Cyril's watchword, and the miaphysite confession. Cyril believed the phrase to be Athanasius's; it is in fact from Apollinaris, which the miaphysite tradition acknowledges while holding that Cyril's use of it is orthodox and Apollinaris's was not."}],dissent:"The Oriental Orthodox: the Coptic Orthodox Church of Alexandria, the Syriac Orthodox Church, the Armenian Apostolic Church, the Ethiopian Orthodox Tewahedo Church, the Eritrean Orthodox Tewahedo Church and the Malankara Orthodox Syrian Church of India — some sixty million Christians today, whose separation begins here. They are miaphysite and expressly not monophysite, and the distinction is not pedantry but the whole substance of the case: they confess one incarnate nature united out of two, divinity and humanity, without confusion, without change, without division and without separation — the same four adverbs — with the humanity entire, consubstantial with ours, and not diminished, absorbed or mixed. To call them monophysite is to accuse them of the doctrine of Eutyches, which they have anathematised for fifteen hundred years. Their objection to Chalcedon was that 'in two natures' names a duality after the union that Cyril had excluded, and that a council which needed a Roman Tome to explain Cyril had already lost him. Armenia was absent from the council, its forces having fought the Persians at Avarayr in that very year; the Armenian Church rejected the Definition formally at the councils of Dvin in the following century, in 506 and again in 554–555.",aftermath:"The imperial attempts to mend it are a history in themselves: Zeno's Henotikon of 482, which papered over the Definition and produced the Acacian schism with Rome instead; Justinian's Three Chapters; the monenergist and monothelite compromises of the seventh century, which failed in their turn; and then the Arab conquests, which removed the question from imperial hands by removing Egypt and Syria from the empire. In the modern period the theological work has gone the other way. The unofficial Pro Oriente consultations from 1971, the common declarations of Paul VI with Shenouda III in 1973 and of John Paul II with Ignatius Zakka I Iwas in 1984, and above all the Agreed Statements of the Joint Commission of the Orthodox and Oriental Orthodox Churches at the Monastery of St Bishoy in 1989 and at Chambésy in 1990, concluded that the two families hold the same christological faith in different terminologies and recommended the lifting of the anathemas. Reception of those statements has been incomplete and is contested — the monasteries of Mount Athos and a number of Orthodox theologians have objected that the difference is real and not merely verbal — and no restoration of communion has followed.",receivedBy:"By the Eastern Orthodox as the fourth of seven, by the Catholic Church as the fourth of twenty-one, and by the Anglican, Lutheran and Reformed traditions as the christological standard, the Definition being cited in the Anglican and Lutheran confessional documents. Not by the Oriental Orthodox; not by the Church of the East, though its christology is in fact closer to the Definition than to what Chalcedon condemned.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Norman P. Tanner (ed.), Decrees of the Ecumenical Councils, vol. 1 (1990)",note:"The Definition and the twenty-eight canons, with the manuscript reading ἐν δύο φύσεσιν and the variant ἐκ δύο noted in the apparatus."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Richard Price and Michael Gaddis, The Acts of the Council of Chalcedon, 3 vols (Translated Texts for Historians, Liverpool, 2005)",note:"The complete acts in English; the sessions in which the fathers argue over which preposition to use are the point of going here."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Leo the Great, Ep. 28 (the Tome to Flavian)",note:"The Latin text acclaimed at the council, and the document the miaphysite objection is aimed at as much as the Definition."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Severus of Antioch, Contra impium Grammaticum; Philalethes",note:"The miaphysite case argued at length by its ablest theologian, who also anathematised Eutyches."},{kind:"lexicon",ref:"Hans Lietzmann, Apollinaris von Laodicea und seine Schule (Tübingen, 1904)",note:"The edition of the Apollinarian fragments that establishes the true provenance of μία φύσις τοῦ θεοῦ λόγου σεσαρκωμένη."},{kind:"study",ref:"Aloys Grillmeier, Christ in Christian Tradition, vol. 2 (in several parts)",note:"Follows the reception of the Definition church by church, Armenia and Dvin included."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Agreed Statements of the Joint Commission for Theological Dialogue between the Orthodox Church and the Oriental Orthodox Churches (Anba Bishoy, 1989; Chambésy, 1990)",note:"The texts that concluded the difference was terminological — read them beside the Athonite objections, since reception of them is exactly what is contested."}]},{slug:"orange-529",name:"The Second Council of Orange",year:529,place:"Arausio, in Provence",convokedBy:"Caesarius of Arles, and confirmed by Pope Boniface II in 531",question:"Whether the first movement toward God is the human being's own.",decision:"That grace precedes everything, including the desire to believe: the beginning of faith and the increase of faith are alike gifts, not achievements. Semi-Pelagianism — the position that the first step is ours and grace completes it — was rejected. But the council also rejected the hardest reading of Augustine, stating explicitly that it does not believe anyone is predestined to evil.",keyTerms:[{term:"initium fidei",native:null,meaning:"The beginning of faith. The whole dispute is whether this first movement is God's gift or the creature's own — and Orange rules that it is God's."},{term:"gratia praeveniens",native:null,meaning:"Prevenient grace, grace that goes before. The term becomes the hinge of Western soteriology and is later the pivot of Wesley's theology as much as of Trent's."}],dissent:"The monks of Lérins and Marseilles — Cassian and Faustus of Riez among them — had argued the other side for a century, out of pastoral fear that a rigorous Augustinianism made preaching pointless. Their concern was never that grace was unnecessary but that it must not make the will a spectator.",aftermath:"Orange settled Western teaching on grace so thoroughly that its canons were then largely forgotten, and were unavailable to the sixteenth-century disputants: neither the Reformers nor the fathers of Trent had the text to hand at the time it would have helped most. It was rediscovered afterwards, and both sides have since claimed it.",receivedBy:"A local council of the Latin West with papal confirmation, binding in the Catholic tradition and widely honoured by the Reformed; not a council of the East, which never framed the question in these terms.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"The canons of the Second Council of Orange, in Charles de Clercq (ed.), Concilia Galliae A. 511–A. 695 (CCSL 148A); numbered text in Denzinger, Enchiridion Symbolorum",note:"The twenty-five canons and the conclusion, which is where the explicit refusal of predestination to evil stands."},{kind:"primary",ref:"John Cassian, Conferences 13",note:"The classic statement of the position later called semi-Pelagian, argued pastorally rather than polemically."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Augustine, De praedestinatione sanctorum and De dono perseverantiae",note:"The two late works addressed to the monks of southern Gaul; the initium fidei is the question they were written to answer."},{kind:"study",ref:"Rebecca Harden Weaver, Divine Grace and Human Agency: A Study of the Semi-Pelagian Controversy (Macon, 1996)",note:"The standard English monograph, and fair to the Gallic side rather than treating it as half-heresy."}]},{slug:"constantinople-ii-553",name:"The Second Council of Constantinople",year:553,place:"Constantinople",convokedBy:"The Emperor Justinian I; presided over by Eutychius of Constantinople, with Pope Vigilius in the city and refusing to attend",question:"Can Chalcedon be reconciled with Cyril by condemning three long-dead Antiochene texts — and may the church condemn a man who died in its peace?",decision:"Yes to the first; and, in effect, yes to the second. The council anathematised the Three Chapters — the person and writings of Theodore of Mopsuestia, the writings of Theodoret against Cyril, and the letter of Ibas of Edessa to Mari the Persian — and issued fourteen anathemas giving Chalcedon a strictly Cyrilline reading: the one hypostasis in whom the natures are united is the pre-existent Word himself, so that one may and must say that one of the Holy Trinity suffered in the flesh. Cyril's Twelve Anathemas were thereby given the conciliar authority they had lacked at Ephesus.",keyTerms:[{term:"tria kephalaia",native:"τρία κεφάλαια",meaning:"The Three Chapters — the word means headings or items, and refers to the three condemnations, not to three books. Much Western confusion followed from misreading it."},{term:"hena tes hagias triados peponthenai sarki",native:"ἕνα τῆς ἁγίας τριάδος πεπονθέναι σαρκί",meaning:"'One of the Holy Trinity suffered in the flesh' — the theopaschite formula. Deeply alarming to Latin ears when the Scythian monks first pressed it, and the key to the whole neo-Chalcedonian synthesis: the subject of the passion is the divine person, in his humanity."},{term:"apokatastasis ton panton",native:"ἀποκατάστασις τῶν πάντων",meaning:"The restoration of all things: the hope, associated with Origen, that every rational creature is at last healed. Whether the fifteen anathemas against Origenism belong to this council or to a synod held just before it — Justinian having already issued an edict against Origen, with a list of anathemas, in his letter to the Patriarch Menas of 543 — is disputed among scholars, and it matters, since the difference is between a conciliar condemnation of universal salvation and an imperial one."}],dissent:"The Latin West, and above all Africa and north Italy. Pope Vigilius, brought to Constantinople under duress, issued a Iudicatum condemning the Chapters in 548, withdrew it under African pressure, refused again in his Constitutum of May 553 — and then capitulated in December, confirming the council he had refused to attend. The Aquileian church broke communion, its bishop taking the title patriarch, and the Schism of the Three Chapters ran until a synod at Pavia around 698; Milan returned somewhat earlier. Nothing survives of it today except the patriarchal title that passed to Venice and the ruins of Grado. The Church of the East, however, took the condemnation of Theodore — the Interpreter, the foundation of its whole exegetical tradition — as confirmation that the imperial church had abandoned the faith.",aftermath:"It failed at what it was for: Egypt and Syria were not reconciled, and within a century were no longer in the empire. Its cost to the papacy was high and long-remembered — a pope had been coerced into reversing a solemn public judgement, and the case of Vigilius was set beside that of Honorius in the minority speeches at Vatican I thirteen centuries later. What it did achieve was real and durable: without the neo-Chalcedonian clarification, the Definition of 451 can be read as leaving two subjects in Christ, and the Byzantine, Latin and modern ecumenical reading of Chalcedon all depend on 553 having said what it said.",receivedBy:"By the Eastern Orthodox as the fifth of seven and by the Catholic Church as the fifth of twenty-one. Not by the Oriental Orthodox — who nonetheless regard its christology with considerable sympathy, since it is Cyril's — and not by the Church of the East, for whom it is the condemnation of their doctor.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Richard Price, The Acts of the Council of Constantinople of 553, 2 vols (Translated Texts for Historians, Liverpool, 2009)",note:"The acts in English with an introduction that argues out whether the fifteen anathemas against Origenism belong to this council at all."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Norman P. Tanner (ed.), Decrees of the Ecumenical Councils, vol. 1 (1990)",note:"The fourteen anathemas, including the theopaschite canon and the condemnation of the Three Chapters."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Justinian, Letter to the Patriarch Menas against Origen (543)",note:"The imperial edict with its own list of anathemas — the alternative home for the Origenist condemnations, which is why the attribution matters."},{kind:"study",ref:"Patrick T. R. Gray, The Defense of Chalcedon in the East (451–553) (Leiden, 1979)",note:"How neo-Chalcedonianism was built, and why 451 could not stand without it."},{kind:"study",ref:"Celia Chazelle and Catherine Cubitt (eds.), The Crisis of the Oikoumene: The Three Chapters and the Failed Quest for Unity in the Sixth-Century Mediterranean (Turnhout, 2007)",note:"The Western refusal treated as its own history, Aquileia and the schism to Pavia included."}]},{slug:"constantinople-iii-681",name:"The Third Council of Constantinople",year:681,place:"Constantinople, in the domed hall of the imperial palace",convokedBy:"The Emperor Constantine IV, in session from November 680 to September 681",question:"Has the one Christ one will and one operation, as the imperial theologians proposed in order to win back the miaphysites — or two?",decision:"Two. The council defined two natural wills and two natural operations in Christ, undivided, unchangeable, inseparable, unconfused; the human will not opposed to the divine but following it and subject to it, willing humanly what the Word wills divinely. It condemned Sergius, Pyrrhus, Paul and Peter of Constantinople, Cyrus of Alexandria, Theodore of Pharan, Macarius of Antioch — and Pope Honorius I of Rome, who in a letter of 634 had written of one will in Christ. Honorius is the only pope condemned by name in the definitive acts of a council the Catholic Church numbers as ecumenical.",keyTerms:[{term:"duo physika thelemata kai duo physikas energeias",native:"δύο φυσικὰ θελήματα καὶ δύο φυσικὰς ἐνεργείας",meaning:"Two natural wills and two natural operations. 'Natural' is the load-bearing word: willing belongs to a nature, not to a person, so a Christ with one will would have one nature."},{term:"energeia",native:"ἐνέργεια",meaning:"Operation, activity, energy — what a nature does. Monenergism, the doctrine of a single divine-human operation, was the earlier and subtler compromise; monothelitism was the retreat from it."},{term:"gnomic will",native:null,meaning:"Maximus the Confessor's distinction between the natural will (θέλημα φυσικόν), which simply wills what its nature is for, and the gnomic will, the deliberative wavering of fallen persons choosing among options. Christ has the first and not the second: he is free without being hesitant."}],dissent:"The condemned side is worth stating as it stated itself, since the council names men and the textbooks rarely give their case. Monenergism, the abler position, held that the one Christ performs a single theandric operation — a phrase drawn from Dionysius the Areopagite and congenial to Cyril's heirs — on the ground that acts belong to the one who acts, and Christ is one; it was framed to make Chalcedon sayable to Egypt and Syria, and for a moment it worked. Monothelitism, the narrower retreat set out in the Ekthesis of 638, held that there is one will in Christ because the human willing of Jesus never willed anything the Word did not will, so that to posit two is to posit a possible opposition inside the saviour. Honorius's letter of 634 is reaching for that pastoral point and not for a denial of Christ's humanity. The council's answer, which is Maximus's, is that willing belongs to nature and not to person: a Christ with no human will has no human nature, and it is exactly the human will, freely consenting in Gethsemane, that had to be healed. Separately, the Maronite Church of Lebanon is associated by most historians with the monothelite settlement, chiefly on the testimony of the tenth-century Melkite chronicler Eutychius of Alexandria and of Maronite sources read against the grain. The Maronite Church itself rejects this account entirely, holding that it has never been separated from the see of Peter nor ever professed one will in Christ, and that the charge originates with its Byzantine rivals. It is a case where the tradition's memory and the historians' reconstruction genuinely diverge, and both should be set down. The Maronite Church is today one of the Eastern Catholic churches, in full communion with Rome, with a patriarch at Bkerké and a liturgy in Syriac.",aftermath:"Maximus the Confessor, who had argued the case for two wills against the whole imperial establishment and been condemned for it, was vindicated posthumously: tried at Constantinople in 655 and again in 662, and by the tradition mutilated in tongue and right hand before being exiled to Lazica, where he died in that year. Pope Martin I, arrested at the Lateran and exiled to Cherson, died there in 655, the last pope venerated as a martyr. The condemnation of Honorius became the standing difficulty for papal claims: Pope Leo II, confirming the council, glossed the sentence as condemning Honorius not for teaching heresy but for failing by negligence to extinguish it — though the surviving versions of Leo's letters are not uniformly that mild — a distinction that the minority at Vatican I found insufficient and the majority found decisive.",receivedBy:"By the Eastern Orthodox as the sixth of seven and by the Catholic Church as the sixth of twenty-one. Not by the Oriental Orthodox or the Church of the East, though neither holds a monothelite christology.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Norman P. Tanner (ed.), Decrees of the Ecumenical Councils, vol. 1 (1990)",note:"The definition of two wills and two operations, and the sentence naming Honorius among the condemned."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Richard Price, The Acts of the Council of Constantinople of 681 (Translated Texts for Historians, Liverpool)",note:"The sessions in English, including the reading of Honorius's letter and Leo II's confirmation."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Maximus the Confessor, Disputation with Pyrrhus and the Opuscula theologica et polemica — selections in Andrew Louth, Maximus the Confessor (London, 1996)",note:"The argument the council adopted, made by a monk with no see, twenty years before it won."},{kind:"study",ref:"Demetrios Bathrellos, The Byzantine Christ: Person, Nature, and Will in the Christology of Saint Maximus the Confessor (Oxford, 2004)",note:"The clearest exposition of why willing is assigned to nature and not to person, and of the gnomic will."},{kind:"study",ref:"Cyril Hovorun, Will, Action and Freedom: Christological Controversies in the Seventh Century (Leiden, 2008)",note:"Takes monenergism seriously as a position rather than as a failed manoeuvre."},{kind:"study",ref:"Matti Moosa, The Maronites in History (Syracuse, 1986)",note:"States the historians' case for a monothelite Maronite past, which the Maronite Church rejects — the divergence the entry sets down is visible in the sources this book uses."}]},{slug:"quinisext-692",name:"The Council in Trullo, called the Quinisext",year:692,place:"Constantinople, in the domed hall of the imperial palace",convokedBy:"The Emperor Justinian II, to supply the canons that the fifth and sixth councils had failed to issue",question:"By what discipline shall the church live — and when the customs of Rome and of Constantinople differ, whose are the church's?",decision:"One hundred and two canons, and Constantinople's customs. Canon 2 fixed the received canonical collection. Canon 13 upheld the marriage of priests and deacons ordained already married, naming and rejecting the contrary Roman rule. Canon 36 renewed Constantinople's equality of privilege with Rome. Canon 55 forbade the Saturday fast of Lent, a Roman practice, under penalty. Canon 67 renewed the apostolic prohibition of blood. Canon 82 required that Christ be depicted in human form rather than as a lamb, so that the icon might confess the incarnation — a canon of the first importance for everything that followed at Nicaea II.",keyTerms:[{term:"penthekte synodos",native:"πενθέκτη σύνοδος",meaning:"The 'fifth-sixth' council: not a council in its own right but a canonical supplement to the fifth and sixth, which had issued doctrine and no discipline. Hence the Latin Quinisextum."},{term:"troullos",native:"τροῦλλος",meaning:"The dome. The council met in the domed hall of the Great Palace, whence 'in Trullo' — the same hall in which the sixth council had sat, which is part of the claim to be its continuation."}],dissent:"Rome. Pope Sergius I refused to sign, saying he would rather die than consent to novelties; Justinian II sent the protospatharius Zacharias to arrest him, the exarch's troops came from Ravenna and the people of Rome rose, and the officer is said to have escaped only by hiding under the pope's own bed. Later popes were more accommodating: Hadrian I and John VIII spoke well of the canons in general terms, and the Latin church came to treat them as usable where they did not contradict Roman usage — which, on the points the canons were framed to contest, meant not at all.",aftermath:"The Trullan canons are the backbone of Orthodox canon law to this day: the married priesthood of the Eastern churches, the reception of converts, the ban on clerics attending the theatre, the regulation of penance, and — through canon 82 — the theology of the icon all rest here. In the West none of it applied, and the divergence in discipline that the canons name became the divergence in life that the two halves of the church actually experienced: a Greek parish priest with a wife and children and a Latin one without.",receivedBy:"This is the exemplary case, and the split runs through the middle of it. The Eastern Orthodox receive it fully, not as a separate council but as part of the sixth, which is why their list still reads seven; the Greek case for Roman reception rests partly on the first canon of Nicaea II, which confirms the canons of the six holy councils and which Rome did receive. The Catholic Church has never received the Trullan canons as ecumenical, holding that Nicaea II's general clause cannot have ratified what Rome had already refused, and accepting individual canons as it finds them tolerable. A single assembly, canonical scripture to one half of Chalcedonian Christendom and a disciplinary curiosity to the other, and numbered by neither as its own. Not received by the Oriental Orthodox or the Church of the East.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"The one hundred and two canons in Trullo — English in Henry R. Percival, The Seven Ecumenical Councils (NPNF, second series, vol. 14)",note:"Canons 2, 13, 36, 55, 67 and 82 in translation, with Beveridge's and Balsamon's notes attached."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Nicodemus the Hagiorite, The Rudder (Pedalion)",note:"The Trullan canons as Orthodox canon law actually reads and applies them, which is the difference between a text and a discipline."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Liber Pontificalis, Life of Sergius I — English in Raymond Davis, The Book of Pontiffs",note:"The Roman account of the arrest attempt and of Zacharias under the bed; it is the papal biographer's version, and there is no other."},{kind:"study",ref:"George Nedungatt and Michael Featherstone (eds.), The Council in Trullo Revisited (Rome, 1995)",note:"Text, translation and essays on the reception question — including whether Nicaea II's first canon can be read as Roman ratification."}]},{slug:"nicaea-ii-787",name:"The Second Council of Nicaea",year:787,place:"Nicaea in Bithynia, the council having been broken up by soldiers at Constantinople the previous year",convokedBy:"The Empress Irene, regent for her son, with the Patriarch Tarasios",question:"Is an image of Christ an idol and a lie, or a confession of the incarnation?",decision:"A confession. The council restored the icons, overturning the council of Hieria of 754, and drew the distinction on which the whole practice rests: to icons of Christ, the Theotokos, the angels and the saints is offered honorific veneration, not the true worship which belongs to the divine nature alone. It quoted Basil — the honour paid to the image passes to the prototype — to explain why venerating the wood is not venerating wood. It also confirmed the canons of the earlier councils, legislated against simony and regulated monastic discipline.",keyTerms:[{term:"timetike proskynesis",native:"τιμητικὴ προσκύνησις",meaning:"Honorific veneration: bowing, kissing, incense, lights. Greek uses προσκύνησις for what one offers to an emperor, a father, a relic or a friend, and the Definition is careful that this is what icons receive."},{term:"alethine latreia",native:"ἀληθινὴ λατρεία",meaning:"True worship, adoration — offered to God alone. The Latin translation sent to Charlemagne's court rendered both Greek words by adoratio, with consequences described below: the Franks read the council as commanding the worship of pictures."},{term:"he tes eikonos time epi to prototypon diabainei",native:"ἡ τῆς εἰκόνος τιμὴ ἐπὶ τὸ πρωτότυπον διαβαίνει",meaning:"'The honour of the image passes over to the prototype' — Basil of Caesarea, On the Holy Spirit 18.45, written about the Son as image of the Father and applied here to painted images."},{term:"eikon",native:"εἰκών",meaning:"Image. The word is Genesis 1:26 and Colossians 1:15 before it is ever a panel of wood: humanity is the image of God, Christ is the image of the invisible God, and the painted icon is the third term in that series, not a fourth thing."}],dissent:"The party the victors called iconoclasts — image-breakers, in Greek εἰκονομάχοι, image-fighters; both are their opponents' names, and they called themselves simply orthodox. Their case deserves better than it usually gets. Beyond the second commandment and the charge of idolatry, Hieria in 754 advanced a properly christological argument: an image can circumscribe only what is circumscribable; an image of Christ therefore either circumscribes the divinity, which is confusion and Eutychianism, or depicts the humanity alone, which is division and Nestorianism; whence the only true image of Christ that he himself instituted is the eucharist. Constantine V argued this at length in his Peuseis. The iconodule answer — that the icon depicts the hypostasis and not the nature, a likeness of the person who is God incarnate — was worked out by John of Damascus, Theodore the Stoudite and the Patriarch Nikephoros precisely because the objection was serious. A second iconoclasm ran from 815 to 843. Today the Reformed churches hold, on the ground of the second commandment and the regulative principle of worship, that images are not to be used in the worship of God; that is a considered and scriptural conviction of living communions, taught in the Heidelberg Catechism, and not a survival of Byzantine iconoclasm.",aftermath:"The restoration of the icons on the first Sunday of Lent in 843 under the Empress Theodora is still kept as the Triumph of Orthodoxy, with the Synodikon read aloud. In the West the council was received by Rome and rejected by the Franks, whose Opus Caroli regis — the Libri Carolini — attacked it on the basis of the faulty Latin translation, and whose Council of Frankfurt in 794 condemned what it took the Greeks to have said. That misunderstanding was eventually cleared, but the West never developed the East's theology of the image, which is one reason the sixteenth century could strip the churches so quickly and with so little theological resistance.",receivedBy:"By the Eastern Orthodox as the seventh and last ecumenical council — the number that defines them — and by the Catholic Church as the seventh of twenty-one. Not numbered by the Oriental Orthodox or the Church of the East, though the Coptic, Ethiopian, Armenian and Syriac traditions all use and venerate images in their own idioms. Lutherans and Anglicans retained images; the Reformed did not.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Richard Price, The Acts of the Second Council of Nicaea (787), 2 vols (Translated Texts for Historians, Liverpool, 2018)",note:"The complete acts in English, including the session in which the Definition of Hieria is quoted clause by clause in order to be refuted."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Norman P. Tanner (ed.), Decrees of the Ecumenical Councils, vol. 1 (1990)",note:"The horos with its distinction of τιμητικὴ προσκύνησις from ἀληθινὴ λατρεία — the distinction the Latin translation collapsed."},{kind:"primary",ref:"John of Damascus, Three Treatises on the Divine Images, trans. Andrew Louth; Theodore the Stoudite, On the Holy Icons, trans. Catharine Roth",note:"The iconodule case worked out before and after the council, against an objection its authors treated as serious."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Ann Freeman (ed.), Opus Caroli regis contra synodum (the Libri Carolini), MGH",note:"The Frankish attack, and the evidence of what the faulty Latin translation had led Charlemagne's theologians to think the Greeks had said."},{kind:"study",ref:"Leslie Brubaker and John Haldon, Byzantium in the Iconoclast Era c. 680–850: A History (Cambridge, 2011)",note:"Argues that iconoclasm was narrower and later than the iconodule sources make it, and that Constantine V's Peuseis survive only as quoted by his enemies."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Heidelberg Catechism, Q&A 96–98",note:"The Reformed position stated confessionally, as a reading of the second commandment rather than as an inheritance from Byzantium."}]},{slug:"photian-869-879",name:"The Photian Councils",year:869,place:"Constantinople, in Hagia Sophia",convokedBy:"The first (869-870) by the Emperor Basil I with Pope Hadrian II's legates; the second (879-880) by Basil I again, with the legates of Pope John VIII",question:"Who has the last word about a patriarch of Constantinople — the see of Rome, or the church in council? And may anything be added to the creed?",decision:"Both councils answered, and they answered differently. That of 869-870 deposed and anathematised Photius, restored Ignatius, and required the signature of a Roman formula asserting the see of Rome's judgement; some hundred and two bishops signed at the end, having begun with twelve. That of 879-880, with some three hundred and eighty bishops and Rome's legates present, annulled the earlier council, restored Photius — Ignatius having died and Photius having been reconciled to him — and decreed that the creed of Nicaea and Constantinople is to be kept without addition or subtraction, anathematising anyone who composes another. Rome's legates subscribed; whether, when and how Rome afterwards withdrew from that subscription is itself contested, and is treated below.",keyTerms:[{term:"filioque",native:null,meaning:"'And from the Son'. The Latin interpolation in the creed, not yet used at Rome in 879 but already sung in Frankish churches, and the subject of Photius's Mystagogy. The council of 879 forbade additions without naming it."},{term:"pentarchia",native:"πενταρχία",meaning:"The pentarchy: the Byzantine constitution of the church as five patriarchates — Rome, Constantinople, Alexandria, Antioch, Jerusalem — of which Rome is first in honour and none is sovereign. Against this, Rome had by now articulated a primacy of jurisdiction."},{term:"oikonomia",native:"οἰκονομία",meaning:"Economy: the Eastern principle by which the strict application of a canon may be relaxed for the good of souls. The whole quarrel began over whether Photius's rapid elevation from layman to patriarch in six days could be accepted by economy, as the East held, or was simply void, as Rome held."}],dissent:"The two churches, from each other, slowly. Neither council caused the schism; together they are its fault line made visible. The mutual excommunications of July 1054 — Cardinal Humbert's bull laid on the altar of Hagia Sophia, and the synodal reply of the Patriarch Michael Keroularios — were narrower in law than in memory, since the bull condemned the patriarch and his adherents rather than the Eastern church, and Pope Leo IX had died three months before it was issued, so that the legates' authority was arguably extinct. What actually ended communion, in the judgement of most historians, was the sack of Constantinople by the Fourth Crusade in 1204 and the Latin patriarchate installed over it. The Eastern Orthodox Church, some two hundred and twenty million strong, exists as a separate communion because of this fault; so, on the other side, does the Catholic Church as a communion defined by the papacy.",aftermath:"The Western portrait of Photius as a schismatic careerist was substantially demolished by the Catholic historian Francis Dvornik, whose Photian Schism of 1948 showed that the so-called second Photian schism never happened, that the story of a later condemnation of Photius by John VIII is a legend, and that Photius died in the communion of Rome. What Rome did with the council of 879-880 afterwards is a separate and still-argued question: its acts passed into the West through the canonists, dropped out of the Latin lists, and the settled Catholic position that the legates' subscription was void or repudiated is a development of the eleventh century and later rather than an act of John VIII's. Photius is a saint of the Orthodox Church and one of the finest scholars of the Middle Ages, whose Bibliotheca preserves our only knowledge of many lost books. The excommunications of 1054 were lifted by the Joint Declaration of Paul VI and the Patriarch Athenagoras I on 7 December 1965, read simultaneously in Rome and Constantinople, which committed both churches to remove them from memory; communion was not restored, and the theological dialogue continues.",receivedBy:"Here the counts diverge outright. The Catholic Church numbers the council of 869-870 as the Eighth Ecumenical Council — a reckoning that appears in Western lists only from the eleventh century, in the period of the reform papacy, whose defenders argue that the delay reflects the slow assembly of the Latin canonical corpus rather than invention. Many Eastern Orthodox regard the council of 879-880 as the eighth, a position urged strongly in the modern period though never formally defined by a pan-Orthodox synod, and hold that 869-870 was annulled by the council that annulled it. The Oriental Orthodox and the Church of the East number neither.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Norman P. Tanner (ed.), Decrees of the Ecumenical Councils, vol. 1 (1990)",note:"The canons of 869–870, which is the council the Catholic list numbers eighth; the acts of 879–880 are not in it, and the absence is the point."},{kind:"primary",ref:"J. D. Mansi, Sacrorum Conciliorum Nova et Amplissima Collectio",note:"Where the acts of 879–880, with the legates' subscriptions and the decree against additions to the creed, are printed."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Photius, Mystagogy of the Holy Spirit",note:"The Greek case against the filioque made by the man the first council deposed, and it is an argument about causality in the Trinity, not about jurisdiction."},{kind:"study",ref:"Francis Dvornik, The Photian Schism: History and Legend (Cambridge, 1948)",note:"The Catholic historian's demolition of the second Photian schism; the entry's claim that Photius died in Rome's communion rests here."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Cornelius Will (ed.), Acta et scripta quae de controversiis ecclesiae graecae et latinae saeculo undecimo composita extant (Leipzig, 1861)",note:"Humbert's bull and the synodal reply of 1054 in their actual wording, which is narrower than the memory of them."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Joint Catholic-Orthodox Declaration of Pope Paul VI and the Patriarch Athenagoras I, 7 December 1965",note:"The text lifting the excommunications, and explicit that it does not restore communion."}]},{slug:"lateran-iv",name:"The Fourth Lateran Council",year:1215,place:"The Lateran basilica, Rome",convokedBy:"Pope Innocent III",question:"What the church of the high Middle Ages would require of every Christian.",decision:"The largest and most consequential council of the medieval West. It defined that the bread and wine are transubstantiated, using that word conciliarly for the first time; required every Christian of years of discretion to confess to their own priest and receive communion at least once a year, at Easter; ordered new preaching and pastoral standards; forbade the founding of further religious orders; and legislated the Fifth Crusade.",keyTerms:[{term:"transsubstantiatio",native:null,meaning:"The substance of the bread and wine changed into the body and blood, the accidents remaining. The Aristotelian frame is doing the technical work, and this council's use of the word is what made it doctrine rather than one theological opinion among several."},{term:"omnis utriusque sexus",native:null,meaning:"“Everyone of both sexes” — the opening words of canon 21, which made annual confession and Easter communion universally obligatory and thereby reshaped the ordinary religious life of Europe more than any other medieval enactment."}],dissent:"The same council imposed distinguishing dress on Jews and Muslims in Christian lands and barred Jews from public office — canons that fed centuries of persecution. It is recorded here because a council is not only its doctrine, and this one's most enduring practical legacy is not one its defenders cite.",aftermath:"Its pastoral canons created the confessional culture of the later Middle Ages and, with it, the manuals, the indulgence economy and eventually the grievances the Reformation began from. Trent reaffirmed transubstantiation against the Reformers three centuries later.",receivedBy:"Numbered as the twelfth ecumenical council by the Catholic Church; not received by the Orthodox, who reject both its authority and its eucharistic terminology.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Norman P. Tanner (ed.), Decrees of the Ecumenical Councils, vol. 1 (1990)",note:"The seventy constitutions in Latin and English — canon 1 for transubstantiatio, canon 21 for Omnis utriusque sexus, canons 68–70 for the Jews."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Solomon Grayzel, The Church and the Jews in the XIIIth Century (Philadelphia, 1933)",note:"Prints and translates the conciliar and papal texts on distinguishing dress and public office, with the documents of their enforcement."},{kind:"study",ref:"Thomas N. Tentler, Sin and Confession on the Eve of the Reformation (Princeton, 1977)",note:"What canon 21 actually produced: the manuals, the confessional practice, and the anxieties the Reformation began from."},{kind:"study",ref:"Colin Morris, The Papal Monarchy: The Western Church from 1050 to 1250 (Oxford, 1989)",note:"Places the council at the summit of the reform papacy rather than treating it as a list of decrees."},{kind:"study",ref:"John C. Moore, Pope Innocent III (Leiden, 2003)",note:"For what Innocent himself intended by the assembly and how far the canons were his own drafting."}]},{slug:"lyons-ii-1274",name:"The Second Council of Lyons",year:1274,place:"Lyons",convokedBy:"Pope Gregory X, with the Emperor Michael VIII Palaiologos sending a delegation",question:"Can the Greek church be reunited with Rome on Rome's terms — the filioque, the primacy, purgatory, unleavened bread — and can an emperor make his church agree?",decision:"The union was proclaimed. The Greek delegation — the former patriarch Germanos III, Theophanes of Nicaea and the Grand Logothete George Akropolites — professed the Roman faith in Michael's name, swore to the papal primacy, and sang the creed with the filioque, the added clause repeated three times. The council also defined purgatory, regulated the conclave in the constitution Ubi periculum after the vacancy of nearly three years that had preceded Gregory's own election, and planned a crusade that never sailed.",keyTerms:[{term:"azyma",native:"ἄζυμα",meaning:"Unleavened bread. The Latins used it and the Greeks did not, and in the eleventh and twelfth centuries this was argued as fiercely as the procession of the Spirit — the Greek case being that leavened bread signifies the ensouled humanity of Christ, and that Christ's own supper was before the feast."},{term:"tanquam ex uno principio",native:null,meaning:"'As from one principle' — the council's formula for the Spirit's procession from Father and Son, intended to answer the Greek objection that the filioque posits two causes in the Trinity."}],dissent:"The Byzantine church, comprehensively. The Patriarch Joseph I refused the union and was deposed, retiring to a monastery; the unionist John XI Bekkos was installed in his place. The union was the emperor's policy and almost nobody else's: Michael VIII had recovered Constantinople in 1261 and needed Rome to restrain Charles of Anjou, who was assembling an army to take it back. Michael's sister Eulogia is reported by Pachymeres to have said that the empire might go to ruin provided the faith did not. When Michael died in 1282 his son Andronikos II repudiated the union at once; the Council of Blachernae in 1285 condemned Bekkos, and Michael was refused Orthodox burial.",aftermath:"Thomas Aquinas died on the road to the council in March 1274 and Bonaventure died during it in July, which gives the assembly a certain melancholy grandeur in the histories. Its enduring achievement was the conclave law, not the union. What Lyons proved — and Florence would prove again at greater cost — is that a union negotiated by emperors and ratified by delegations cannot be imposed on a church that has not been persuaded, and that in the East the faithful and the monks, not the hierarchy alone, hold the veto.",receivedBy:"By the Catholic Church as the fourteenth ecumenical council. Rejected by the Orthodox Church, which regards it as a council of the Latins at which some Greeks were present under political compulsion. Not numbered by the Oriental Orthodox or the Church of the East.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Norman P. Tanner (ed.), Decrees of the Ecumenical Councils, vol. 1 (1990)",note:"Michael VIII's profession of faith, the definition on purgatory, and the conclave constitution Ubi periculum."},{kind:"primary",ref:"George Pachymeres, Historia (De Michaele et Andronico Palaeologis)",note:"The Byzantine narrative of the union and its collapse, and the source for Eulogia's remark — a hostile witness writing after the repudiation."},{kind:"study",ref:"Deno J. Geanakoplos, Emperor Michael Palaeologus and the West, 1258–1282 (Cambridge, Mass., 1959)",note:"The standard account of why Michael needed the union and of what Charles of Anjou was assembling while it was negotiated."},{kind:"study",ref:"Aristeides Papadakis, Crisis in Byzantium: The Filioque Controversy in the Patriarchate of Gregory II of Cyprus (New York, 1983)",note:"Covers the repudiation, Blachernae 1285 and the fall of Bekkos — the aftermath rather than the council."}]},{slug:"hesychast-councils",name:"The Hesychast Councils of Constantinople",year:1341,place:"Constantinople",convokedBy:"Emperors Andronikos III and, for the later sessions, John VI Kantakouzenos",question:"Whether the light seen by the praying monks is God himself, or something created.",decision:"Three sessions across a decade — 1341, 1347 and 1351 — upheld Gregory Palamas and the monks of Athos against Barlaam of Calabria and his successors. The councils affirmed that God's essence is forever unknowable while his energies are truly God and truly participable, so that the light of Tabor is uncreated and deification is real participation rather than a figure of speech.",keyTerms:[{term:"ousia",native:"οὐσία",meaning:"Essence — which no creature knows or shares, on this teaching, at any level of sanctity whatever."},{term:"energeiai",native:"ἐνέργειαι",meaning:"Operations, energies. Not created effects and not the essence: God as he gives himself, in which a human being genuinely participates."},{term:"hesychia",native:"ἡσυχία",meaning:"Stillness. The practice the councils were convened to judge — ceaseless invocation of the Name with the mind descended into the heart."}],dissent:"Barlaam mocked the monks as omphalopsychoi, navel-souls, and argued that any claim to see God directly was either blasphemy or delusion; Gregory Akindynos and Nikephoros Gregoras carried the opposition after him. Their objection was philosophical rather than devotional: that a real distinction in God threatens divine simplicity.",aftermath:"Palamite theology became the settled framework of Orthodox spirituality, and Palamas is commemorated on the second Sunday of Great Lent. The Latin West, working with a different account of simplicity, has largely not accepted the distinction, and it remains one of the deepest live differences between the traditions that is not about authority.",receivedBy:"Held as binding by the Orthodox churches, which count these among their major councils; not received in the Catholic West, though contemporary Catholic scholarship treats the distinction more sympathetically than it once did.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"The Hagioretic Tome (1340) and the synodal tomes of 1341, 1347 and 1351",note:"The conciliar texts themselves, in which the essence–energies distinction is stated as the church's teaching rather than as Palamas's opinion."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Gregory Palamas, Triads in Defence of the Holy Hesychasts; English selection trans. Nicholas Gendle (Classics of Western Spirituality)",note:"The defence written while the case was still open, and the source of nearly everything the tomes then ratified."},{kind:"lexicon",ref:"Jean Gouillard, 'Le Synodikon de l'Orthodoxie: édition et commentaire,' Travaux et Mémoires 2 (1967)",note:"The critical edition of the text read on the Sunday of Orthodoxy, where the Palamite additions of 1351 were inserted."},{kind:"study",ref:"John Meyendorff, A Study of Gregory Palamas (London, 1964)",note:"The book that made Palamas a subject of Western scholarship; its reading of Barlaam has since been questioned."},{kind:"study",ref:"Norman Russell, Gregory Palamas and the Making of Palamism in the Modern Age (Oxford, 2019)",note:"Distinguishes what the councils defined from what twentieth-century Orthodox theology built on it, and surveys the Catholic reassessment."}]},{slug:"constance",name:"The Council of Constance",year:1414,place:"Constance, on the Bodensee",convokedBy:"The antipope John XXIII under pressure from Emperor Sigismund",question:"Who is pope, when three men claim to be — and whether a council may answer that.",decision:"It ended the Great Western Schism: two claimants deposed, the third persuaded to resign, and Martin V elected in 1417. To do so it asserted, in the decree Haec sancta, that a general council holds authority immediately from Christ and that everyone including the pope must obey it in matters of faith and reform — the high-water mark of conciliarism. The decree Frequens then required councils to meet at regular intervals.",keyTerms:[{term:"Haec sancta",native:null,meaning:"The 1415 decree asserting a council's authority over a pope. Whether it is a binding conciliar definition or an emergency measure of contested standing is still argued by Catholic theologians, and the answer bears directly on how papal primacy is understood."},{term:"salvus conductus",native:null,meaning:"The safe conduct under which Jan Hus travelled to the council. He was arrested, tried and burned anyway, on the principle that no faith need be kept with a heretic — a decision that outraged Bohemia and precipitated fifteen years of war."}],dissent:"Hus refused to recant what he had not been shown to have written, and was burned on 6 July 1415; Jerome of Prague followed in 1416. Wycliffe, dead thirty years, was condemned and his bones ordered exhumed and burned. The Hussite wars that followed were among the first in which a religious dissent successfully defended itself by force.",aftermath:"The schism ended, but conciliarism did not survive the century: the popes reasserted supremacy, and Vatican I in 1870 defined papal infallibility against everything Haec sancta implied. In 1999 a Catholic-Lutheran commission and later a papal expression of regret addressed the killing of Hus; the Czech churches keep 6 July still.",receivedBy:"Counted as the sixteenth ecumenical council by the Catholic Church, though only for its later sessions — an important qualification, since it is precisely the earlier ones that contain Haec sancta.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Norman P. Tanner (ed.), Decrees of the Ecumenical Councils, vol. 1 (1990)",note:"Haec sancta and Frequens printed with the rest, and Tanner's headnote states plainly which sessions the Catholic Church receives."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Peter of Mladoňovice, Account of the Trial and Condemnation of Master Jan Hus — in Matthew Spinka, John Hus at the Council of Constance (New York, 1965)",note:"An eyewitness record by a member of Hus's own party; partisan, and the fullest account there is."},{kind:"study",ref:"Brian Tierney, Foundations of the Conciliar Theory (Cambridge, 1955)",note:"Traces conciliarism to the canonists rather than to the emergency, which is what makes Haec sancta hard to dismiss as a mere expedient."},{kind:"study",ref:"Francis Oakley, The Conciliarist Tradition: Constitutionalism in the Catholic Church 1300–1870 (Oxford, 2003)",note:"For the argument, still unfinished among Catholic theologians, over the standing of the decree."},{kind:"study",ref:"Phillip H. Stump, The Reforms of the Council of Constance (Leiden, 1994)",note:"The reform work that is usually forgotten in favour of the schism and the burnings."}]},{slug:"florence-1439",name:"The Council of Ferrara-Florence",year:1439,place:"Opened at Ferrara in January 1438 and moved to Florence in January 1439; later sessions at Rome from 1443",convokedBy:"Pope Eugene IV, in rivalry with the conciliarist fathers still sitting at Basel, with the Emperor John VIII Palaiologos and the Patriarch Joseph II present in person — Joseph dying at Florence on 10 June 1439, a month before the union was signed",question:"The same four questions as at Lyons, but this time argued out in full session by the best theologians both churches had, with the Turks at the walls.",decision:"Union, in the bull Laetentur caeli of 6 July 1439 — the incipit Cyril had used to Antioch a thousand years before: that the Holy Spirit proceeds eternally from the Father and the Son as from one principle, the Latin filioque and the Greek 'through the Son' being declared to signify the same thing; that both leavened and unleavened bread are valid matter; that souls are purified after death; and that the Roman pontiff holds the primacy over the whole church, saving the privileges and rights of the Eastern patriarchs. Further bulls of union followed with the Armenians in 1439, with the Copts in 1442 in the bull Cantate Domino, with the Syrians in 1444, and with the Chaldeans and Maronites of Cyprus in 1445.",keyTerms:[{term:"dia tou huiou",native:"διὰ τοῦ υἱοῦ",meaning:"'Through the Son' — the Greek patristic formula, used by Cyril and Maximus, which the council declared equivalent in meaning to the Latin 'and from the Son'. Whether it is equivalent is precisely what is still argued."},{term:"epiklesis",native:"ἐπίκλησις",meaning:"The invocation of the Holy Spirit over the gifts. The Greeks held the consecration to be effected by it, the Latins by the words of institution; the council's decree for the Armenians came down on the Latin side, and the question remains open in the dialogues."},{term:"Laetentur caeli",native:null,meaning:"'Let the heavens rejoice' — the bull of union, read in Latin by Cesarini and in Greek by Bessarion in the cathedral of Florence, and repudiated within a few years by nearly everyone who had signed it."}],dissent:"Mark Eugenikos, Metropolitan of Ephesus, alone among the Greek bishops present refused to sign — the emperor is said to have remarked, on being told, that nothing had been done. He is venerated as a saint and confessor by the Orthodox Church. The union was rejected by the clergy and people of Constantinople on the delegation's return; several signatories publicly recanted. The historian Doukas attributes to the Grand Duke Loukas Notaras the saying that it were better to see the Turkish turban in the midst of the city than the Latin mitre; the attribution is traditional and its authenticity is doubted by many scholars, and it should not be taken as evidence of what Notaras thought. The union was proclaimed in Hagia Sophia on 12 December 1452 by Cardinal Isidore of Kiev, to a church half-empty in protest. Constantinople fell on 29 May 1453. A synod at Constantinople in 1484 formally repudiated Florence.",aftermath:"The union failed, and the promised military help was negligible; the crusade that did set out was destroyed at Varna in 1444. Yet Florence is not simply a failure. Its decrees became the canonical foundation for several of the Eastern Catholic churches, whose existence — Ukrainian, Melkite, Chaldean, Syro-Malabar and others — the Orthodox and the Assyrians regard as the chief remaining obstacle to reunion, and which the Second Vatican Council affirmed in their own rites. Its bull for the Copts, Cantate Domino, also contains the harshest formulation in any conciliar text of the doctrine that none outside the Catholic Church can be saved, a text much reinterpreted by later Catholic teaching and finally recast at Vatican II.",receivedBy:"By the Catholic Church as the seventeenth ecumenical council, listed there as Basel-Ferrara-Florence. Repudiated by the Orthodox Church and by the Oriental Orthodox and Assyrian churches whose delegations signed its later bulls; in every case the signing hierarchs were disowned at home. It is the high-water mark of the attempt to reunite Christendom by document.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Norman P. Tanner (ed.), Decrees of the Ecumenical Councils, vol. 1 (1990)",note:"Laetentur caeli and the later bulls of union, Cantate Domino among them, in Latin and English."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Concilium Florentinum: Documenta et Scriptores (Rome)",note:"The critical edition of the acts and associated documents, Greek and Latin, on which all modern work on the council rests."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Sylvester Syropoulos, Memoirs",note:"The council from inside the Greek delegation, by a man who signed and regretted it; the standard corrective to the official acts."},{kind:"study",ref:"Joseph Gill, The Council of Florence (Cambridge, 1959)",note:"The standard history, written by a Catholic scholar and generally judged fair to the Greek case."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Doukas, Historia — English in Harry J. Magoulias, Decline and Fall of Byzantium to the Ottoman Turks (Detroit, 1975)",note:"Where the turban-and-mitre saying is attributed to Notaras; the attribution is Doukas's, decades after the fact, and doubted."}]},{slug:"trent-1545-63",name:"The Council of Trent",year:1545,place:"Trent in the Tyrol, with a period at Bologna; twenty-five sessions over eighteen years",convokedBy:"Pope Paul III, and continued under Julius III and Pius IV, after twenty-five years of delay that the reformers had cited as evidence of bad faith",question:"What does the Catholic Church actually hold on scripture, tradition, justification and the sacraments — and what will it do about the abuses that provoked the schism?",decision:"On scripture: the canon fixed to include the deuterocanonical books, and the Latin Vulgate declared the authentic text for public use. On tradition: the gospel is contained in written books and unwritten traditions, both to be received with equal affection of piety and reverence — the drafting change from 'partly in the scriptures, partly in traditions' to a simple 'and' being one of the most consequential edits in the history of doctrine, and the opening through which twentieth-century Catholic theology recovered the primacy of scripture. On justification, in session six of 1547: sixteen chapters and thirty-three canons holding that justification is not only the remission of sins but the sanctification and renewal of the inner man; that it is wholly gratuitous and prevenient, no one being able to merit its beginning; that faith is its root and foundation but that a faith without hope and charity does not unite one to Christ; that the justified may truly merit an increase of grace; and that no one may be certain with the certainty of faith that he is among the elect. Seven sacraments were defined, transubstantiation affirmed in 1551, the sacrifice of the Mass in 1562. And the reforms: bishops obliged to reside in their dioceses, a seminary required in every diocese, clandestine marriage abolished by the decree Tametsi.",keyTerms:[{term:"pari pietatis affectu ac reverentia",native:null,meaning:"'With equal affection of piety and reverence' — how scripture and apostolic traditions are to be received. Not 'equal authority', and not 'partly-partly'; the wording was fought over and it matters."},{term:"transubstantiatio",native:null,meaning:"Transubstantiation: the term the council calls most apt for the conversion of the whole substance of bread into the body, the species alone remaining. The definition affirms the fact and canonises the term without canonising Aristotle's physics — a distinction later theology has leaned on heavily."},{term:"sola fide",native:null,meaning:"Faith alone: rejected in canon 9 of session six, in the specific sense that nothing else is required by way of cooperation or preparation. What Trent condemned and what Luther meant by the phrase are not obviously the same proposition, which is the whole premise of the 1999 Joint Declaration."},{term:"anathema sit",native:null,meaning:"'Let him be anathema' — the canonical form of Trent's canons, which run to well over a hundred across the sessions, thirty-three on justification alone. Trent's own theologians understood these as condemning propositions rather than persons, a reading that made later rapprochement possible."}],dissent:"The Protestant churches, who were not there. Protestants were invited and a small Lutheran delegation appeared briefly in 1552, but the terms — no vote, and a council already sitting under papal presidency — made participation impossible, and the reformers' demand for a free general council in Germany judged by scripture alone had by then hardened into a demand no pope could grant. What exists today because of the refusal is the whole Protestant world: some eight hundred million Christians in the Lutheran, Reformed, Anglican, Anabaptist, Baptist, Methodist, Pentecostal and independent traditions.",aftermath:"Trent gave the Catholic Church four centuries of doctrinal definition, the Roman Missal of 1570 and the Roman Catechism, a reformed and educated clergy, and an identity defined against Protestantism — the last of which Vatican II set about undoing. On justification, the extraordinary sequel is the Joint Declaration on the Doctrine of Justification signed by the Lutheran World Federation and the Catholic Church at Augsburg on Reformation Day 1999, which found a consensus in basic truths such that the sixteenth-century condemnations do not apply to the partner's teaching as there presented. It was joined by the World Methodist Council in 2006, the Anglican Consultative Council in 2016 and the World Communion of Reformed Churches in 2017. Its reception is contested: more than a hundred and sixty German Protestant theology professors published objections before the signing, holding that the agreement obscured a real difference, and no intercommunion has followed.",receivedBy:"By the Catholic Church as the nineteenth ecumenical council, and by no one else. The Orthodox, Oriental Orthodox and Assyrian churches do not receive it; the Protestant churches were formed by rejecting what it defends. From Trent onward the councils in the list are Catholic councils, and the word ecumenical, in this series, means something narrower than it did at Nicaea.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Norman P. Tanner (ed.), Decrees of the Ecumenical Councils, vol. 2 (1990)",note:"All twenty-five sessions in Latin and English, decrees and canons together, which is the only way the anathemas read as intended."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Concilium Tridentinum: Diariorum, Actorum, Epistularum, Tractatuum Nova Collectio (Görres-Gesellschaft, Freiburg)",note:"The drafts and diaries; the change from 'partly–partly' to 'and' in the decree on tradition can be watched happening here."},{kind:"lexicon",ref:"Denzinger, Enchiridion Symbolorum",note:"The canons in the numbering everyone cites, which is how Trent is actually referred to in later theological argument."},{kind:"study",ref:"Hubert Jedin, A History of the Council of Trent (English trans., 2 vols; German original in 4)",note:"The standard history; the English stops before the final period, and later sessions must be sought elsewhere."},{kind:"study",ref:"John W. O'Malley, Trent: What Happened at the Council (Cambridge, Mass., 2013)",note:"A short modern account, particularly on why the Protestant delegation's participation was never really possible."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Joint Declaration on the Doctrine of Justification (Lutheran World Federation and the Catholic Church, 1999), with the Official Common Statement and Annex",note:"The text of the consensus and its careful limits; the Annex exists because the first draft was judged insufficient by objectors on both sides."}]},{slug:"vatican-i-1870",name:"The First Vatican Council",year:1870,place:"Rome, in the north transept of St Peter's",convokedBy:"Pope Pius IX, opening on 8 December 1869 with some seven hundred bishops, and suspended when Italian troops entered Rome",question:"Can God be known by reason, and can the church be governed — specifically, is the Roman pontiff infallible when he defines a doctrine of faith or morals for the whole church?",decision:"Yes to both. The constitution Dei Filius of April 1870 taught that God, the beginning and end of all things, can be known with certainty by the natural light of human reason from created things, and that faith and reason can never truly conflict — a defence of reason against the fideism and traditionalism of the age as much as against rationalism. The constitution Pastor aeternus of 18 July defined the pope's primacy of jurisdiction as ordinary, immediate and episcopal over every church and every member, and defined that when the Roman pontiff speaks ex cathedra — that is, when as pastor and teacher of all Christians he defines by his supreme apostolic authority a doctrine of faith or morals to be held by the whole church — he possesses that infallibility with which the divine Redeemer willed his church to be endowed, and such definitions are irreformable of themselves and not from the consent of the church.",keyTerms:[{term:"ex cathedra",native:null,meaning:"'From the chair'. The conditions are strict and the definition narrow: the pope must be acting as universal pastor, defining, on faith or morals, for the whole church. On the strictest reckoning the power has been exercised once since 1870, in the definition of the Assumption in 1950."},{term:"ex sese, non autem ex consensu Ecclesiae",native:null,meaning:"'Of themselves and not from the consent of the church' — the phrase that most alarmed the minority, and the Orthodox, since it appears to sever the pope's teaching from reception by the body. Catholic theology since has laboured to show that it excludes juridical ratification, not the church's assent."},{term:"Honorius",native:null,meaning:"Not a term but a name, and the minority's chief historical weapon: a pope condemned by the Sixth Ecumenical Council. Bishop Hefele of Rottenburg, the greatest historian of the councils then living, was among the opponents, and knew the acts better than anyone in the room."}],dissent:"The minority was substantial and it held, for the most part, not that the doctrine was false but that its definition was inopportune and its historical difficulties unresolved. At the trial vote of 13 July 1870, of some six hundred voting, 451 gave placet, 88 non placet and 62 placet iuxta modum — a conditional yes covering both those who wanted the text strengthened and those who wanted it softened. Hefele, Strossmayer, Dupanloup, Darboy and the American Kenrick were among them. Rather than vote against the pope in public session, most of the minority left Rome before 18 July, when the constitution passed 533 to two, the two being Aloisio Riccio of Cajazzo and Edward Fitzgerald of Little Rock; all submitted afterwards. The refusal that lasted came from the theologians: Ignaz von Döllinger, the foremost Catholic church historian in Germany, refused to accept the definition and was excommunicated in 1871. From that refusal came the Old Catholic Churches, organised under the Declaration of Utrecht in 1889 and existing today as the Union of Utrecht, in communion with the Anglican churches since 1931.",aftermath:"The council was suspended on 20 September 1870 when the Italian army breached the Porta Pia and the temporal power ended; it was never resumed and was formally closed only in 1960, by John XXIII, so that Vatican II might begin. Its work was therefore half-finished: it had defined the papacy without defining the episcopate, and the missing half is precisely what Vatican II supplied in its doctrine of collegiality. For the Orthodox and Old Catholics the definition remains the central obstacle; John Paul II's encyclical Ut unum sint of 1995 invited the other churches to help him find a way of exercising the primacy that all could accept, an invitation that has produced a substantial and continuing literature.",receivedBy:"By the Catholic Church as the twentieth ecumenical council. Rejected by the Orthodox, the Oriental Orthodox, the Assyrians, the Old Catholics and the Protestant churches. The Catholic count of twenty-one long predates 1870, but 1870 is where the authority to convoke, confirm and close a council is located explicitly in the papacy — which is why, for everyone outside that communion, the count and the definition stand or fall together.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Norman P. Tanner (ed.), Decrees of the Ecumenical Councils, vol. 2 (1990)",note:"Dei Filius and Pastor aeternus in full — the ex sese clause reads differently when the surrounding chapters are in front of you."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Acta et decreta sacrorum conciliorum recentiorum (Collectio Lacensis), Freiburg",note:"The conciliar acts and the speeches, including the minority's historical objections and the trial vote of 13 July."},{kind:"study",ref:"Cuthbert Butler, The Vatican Council 1869–1870",note:"Built largely on Bishop Ullathorne's letters; still the most readable narrative of how the minority behaved and why it left."},{kind:"study",ref:"Klaus Schatz, Papal Primacy: From Its Origins to the Present (Collegeville, 1996)",note:"Sets the definition inside the long history of the primacy and is candid about the Honorius problem."},{kind:"study",ref:"John W. O'Malley, Vatican I: The Council and the Making of the Ultramontane Church (Cambridge, Mass., 2018)",note:"The most recent account, on the nineteenth-century politics that made an inopportune definition seem urgent."},{kind:"primary",ref:"The Declaration of Utrecht (1889); John Paul II, Ut unum sint (1995)",note:"The two documents at either end of the aftermath: the refusal that became a church, and the invitation to help reshape the office."}]},{slug:"vatican-ii-1965",name:"The Second Vatican Council",year:1962,place:"Rome, in the nave of St Peter's",convokedBy:"Pope John XXIII, who announced it in January 1959 to the astonishment of his curia; opened 11 October 1962, completed under Paul VI and closed 8 December 1965, with more than two thousand bishops from every continent",question:"How does a church that does not change its faith speak to a world that has changed entirely?",decision:"In four constitutions and twelve further documents. Sacrosanctum Concilium reformed the liturgy and permitted the vernacular. Lumen Gentium described the church first as mystery and as the people of God and only then as hierarchy, restored the doctrine of episcopal collegiality that 1870 had left unsaid, and taught that the one Church of Christ subsists in the Catholic Church, while elements of sanctification and truth are found outside her — a formula that replaced a simple identification and made Catholic ecumenism possible. Dei Verbum set scripture and tradition as flowing from one divine wellspring and put the scriptures at the centre of Catholic life. Gaudium et Spes addressed the modern world in its own terms. Beyond them: Unitatis Redintegratio on ecumenism, with its recognition of separated brethren and its hierarchy of truths; Nostra Aetate, which repudiated the charge of deicide against the Jewish people and rejected every persecution; Dignitatis Humanae on religious freedom as a right grounded in human dignity; and Orientalium Ecclesiarum affirming the Eastern Catholic churches in their own traditions.",keyTerms:[{term:"aggiornamento",native:null,meaning:"Bringing up to date — John XXIII's word for what he wanted, paired always with the deeper ressourcement, the return to the sources, which is what the theologians of the preceding generation had actually done."},{term:"subsistit in",native:null,meaning:"'Subsists in' (Lumen Gentium 8): the Church of Christ subsists in the Catholic Church. The draft had read 'is'; the change is three letters and it reordered the Catholic Church's relation to every other Christian body."},{term:"hierarchia veritatum",native:null,meaning:"The hierarchy of truths (Unitatis Redintegratio 11): doctrines differ in their relation to the foundation of the faith. Not all disagreements are equally church-dividing — the single most fruitful sentence in modern ecumenism."}],dissent:"Archbishop Marcel Lefebvre, one of the council fathers, refused the documents on religious liberty and ecumenism and the reformed liturgy, and founded the Society of Saint Pius X in 1970. He consecrated four bishops without papal mandate in 1988 and incurred excommunication; the excommunications of the four were remitted in 2009, but the Society's canonical situation remains irregular and the doctrinal discussions unresolved. The Society understands itself not as a schism but as preserving the perennial faith and the Roman rite against a rupture it locates in the council itself, and holds that a state of necessity justified the consecrations. Smaller sedevacantist groups hold that the Roman see has been vacant since the council. On the other side, a number of Catholics have held that the council's reforms did not go far enough. The interpretative question was framed by Benedict XVI in his address to the Roman Curia in December 2005 as a choice between a hermeneutic of discontinuity and rupture and one of reform in continuity, and that framing is itself now argued over.",aftermath:"The council is sixty years past and its reception is not finished — which is normal: Nicaea took fifty-six years to reach 381, Chalcedon is still being received in the dialogues, and Trent's liturgical books took a generation to arrive in the parishes. What is not in doubt is the change in the family's manners. Orthodox and Protestant observers sat in St Peter's throughout — the Russian Orthodox from the first session, Constantinople from the third — and the mutual anathemas of 1054 were lifted on the day before the council closed. Every bilateral dialogue in this page's later paragraphs, with the Assyrians in 1994, with the Oriental Orthodox from 1971, with the Lutherans in 1999, runs through the door that Unitatis Redintegratio opened.",receivedBy:"By the Catholic Church as the twenty-first ecumenical council — the current end of the count. Not received as a council by anyone else, and not claiming to be: it is the first council in the series that explicitly addressed the other churches as churches rather than as errors, and its ecumenical significance lies in that address rather than in its number.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Norman P. Tanner (ed.), Decrees of the Ecumenical Councils, vol. 2 (1990)",note:"All sixteen documents in Latin and English; subsistit in and hierarchia veritatum should be read in their own paragraphs, not in quotation."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Acta Synodalia Sacrosancti Concilii Oecumenici Vaticani II (Vatican City, 1970–80)",note:"The drafts and the debates; the change from est to subsistit in, and what the drafters said it meant, is documented here and nowhere else."},{kind:"study",ref:"Giuseppe Alberigo (ed.), History of Vatican II, English ed. Joseph A. Komonchak, 5 vols (Maryknoll and Leuven, 1995–2006)",note:"The fullest history; its own reading has been contested as favouring the discontinuity account, which is part of the argument it describes."},{kind:"study",ref:"John W. O'Malley, What Happened at Vatican II (Cambridge, Mass., 2008)",note:"One volume on the council as an event, and explicit that its style of speech was itself a decision."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Benedict XVI, Address to the Roman Curia, 22 December 2005",note:"The speech that framed the interpretative question as rupture against reform in continuity — the framing now argued over in its turn."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Marcel Lefebvre, Declaration of 21 November 1974",note:"The refusal in his own words, before the consecrations and before the canonical penalties, which is the fairest place to take his case from."}]}],disputes:[{slug:"quartodeciman",name:"The Quartodeciman Controversy",span:"c. 155 – 325, and still unfinished",parties:"The churches of Asia Minor, keeping the Pascha on the fourteenth day of the moon whatever the weekday, against Rome and most others, keeping it on the following Sunday. Quartodeciman, fourteenther, is their opponents' word for them; they described themselves simply as keeping the tradition of John.",question:"On what day is the Pascha kept — and may the bishop of one church compel the custom of another?",course:["The Asian practice was to keep the Pascha on 14 Nisan, when the lambs were killed, following the chronology of John's gospel in which Christ dies as the passover is prepared; the observance was a single night of fast and fulfilment, passion and resurrection held together, and its great monument is the Peri Pascha of Melito of Sardis. The Roman practice keyed the feast instead to the day of the resurrection, and so to the Sunday, of which every Sunday of the year is a weekly echo. Both are defensible; neither is obviously derivative.","Around 155 Polycarp of Smyrna, an old man who had known John, came to Rome and discussed the matter with Bishop Anicetus. Irenaeus, whose account Eusebius preserves, says that neither could persuade the other, that Anicetus invited Polycarp to celebrate the eucharist in his own church as a mark of honour, and that they parted in peace. This is the model of difference without division, and the church has cited it ever since without much imitating it.","Forty years later Victor of Rome tried a different method. Synods were held across the empire and reported for the Sunday observance; Polycrates of Ephesus replied on behalf of the Asian churches, listing the great luminaries who sleep in Asia — Philip and his daughters, John who leaned on the Lord's breast, Polycarp, Melito — and saying that he had gone through all holy scripture and was not frightened by terrifying words, for those greater than he had said that one must obey God rather than men. Victor attempted to cut the Asian churches from communion. Irenaeus of Lyons, who agreed with Victor about the date, wrote to rebuke him for the attempt, reminding him that his own predecessors had kept the peace with those who differed; and the sentence, whatever its formal status, was not sustained.","Nicaea settled the calendar in 325, directing that all should keep the feast together on the Sunday and, in the emperor's covering letter, that the church should have nothing in common with the custom of the Jews in the matter. That framing was not incidental and should not be passed over: the unification of the Christian calendar was argued in part as a separation from the synagogue, and the language Constantine used about it is ugly."],outcome:"Rome's date prevailed and Rome's method did not, which is roughly the shape of the next eighteen centuries. Quartodeciman communities persisted in Asia and Syria for generations. The calendar itself has never stopped moving: the Gregorian reform of 1582 divided Western from Eastern reckoning, so that most Orthodox churches keep a Pascha that usually falls on a different Sunday; the Congress of Constantinople in 1923 produced a revised calendar that some Orthodox churches adopted for fixed feasts and none for Pascha, generating the Old Calendarist separations; and the Aleppo consultation of 1997 proposed a common date on astronomical calculation, which nobody has adopted. In 2025, the seventeen-hundredth anniversary of Nicaea, the two dates coincided on 20 April, and the appeals for a permanent common date were renewed and remain unanswered.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Eusebius, Historia ecclesiastica V.23–25",note:"Preserves Polycrates' letter, the account of Polycarp with Anicetus and Irenaeus's rebuke of Victor — almost everything known about the controversy passes through these chapters, and through a partisan of the Sunday observance."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Melito of Sardis, On Pascha — ed. and trans. Stuart George Hall, Melito of Sardis: On Pascha and Fragments (Oxford Early Christian Texts, 1979)",note:"The Asian observance from inside, showing that keeping the fourteenth day was a theology of passover fulfilled and not merely a preference about dates."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Eusebius, Life of Constantine III.17–20 — English in Averil Cameron and Stuart G. Hall, Eusebius: Life of Constantine (Oxford, 1999)",note:"The emperor's covering letter on the Easter decision, which is where the language about having nothing in common with the Jews actually stands."},{kind:"lexicon",ref:"Venance Grumel, La chronologie (Traité d'études byzantines I, Paris, 1958)",note:"The reference work for the paschal computations themselves, without which the Julian and Gregorian divergence cannot be followed in detail."},{kind:"study",ref:"Paul F. Bradshaw and Lawrence A. Hoffman (eds.), Passover and Easter: Origin and History to Modern Times (Notre Dame, 1999)",note:"For the relation of the Christian Pascha to the Jewish festival, which is the question lying underneath the quarrel about the day."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Towards a Common Date for Easter — World Council of Churches and Middle East Council of Churches consultation, Aleppo, 5–10 March 1997",note:"The modern proposal in its own words, and the document to read for why a shared astronomical reckoning has so far been adopted by nobody."}]},{slug:"rebaptism",name:"Cyprian against Stephen: the Baptism of Heretics",span:"255 – 258",parties:"Cyprian of Carthage with the African episcopate, joined by Firmilian of Caesarea in Cappadocia, against Stephen I of Rome.",question:"Is baptism administered outside the church a real baptism, or nothing at all?",course:["Cyprian's argument is entirely consistent and follows from his ecclesiology. Baptism confers the Spirit; the Spirit is given in the church; there is one church, and outside it no Spirit, no baptism and no salvation. He put it in the sharpest possible terms — that a man cannot have God for his father who has not the church for his mother — and drew the conclusion that a convert from schism or heresy comes to the church unbaptised and must be baptised, since what he received before was not a defective sacrament but no sacrament. Three African councils backed him, the last on 1 September 256 with eighty-seven bishops, each giving his sentence in turn; the preface to that record insists that no one among them sets himself up as a bishop of bishops or compels his colleagues by tyrannical terror, a remark generally read as aimed at Rome.","Stephen answered with an appeal to custom rather than to theory: let nothing be innovated beyond what has been handed down. Converts were to be received by the laying on of hands in penance. The implicit principle — that the sacrament is Christ's act and not the minister's, and so does not depend on the minister's standing — was not fully articulated for another century and a half, but Stephen had hold of it.","The exchange grew savage. Firmilian's letter, preserved among Cyprian's correspondence, is the fiercest attack on Roman pretension to survive from the third century, accusing Stephen of cutting himself off from the unity he claims to guard. Stephen appears to have threatened or broken communion with the Africans and the Cappadocians. Then the persecution under Valerian fell on all of them: Stephen died in 257, Cyprian was beheaded outside Carthage on 14 September 258, and the quarrel simply stopped."],outcome:"Rome's practice became Western law, and Augustine gave it its theory a century and a half later against the Donatists: a baptism given with the right matter, form and intention is valid wherever given, though it is unfruitful outside the church's charity until the recipient is reconciled. That distinction between validity and fruitfulness is one of the most consequential tools Western theology possesses, and every modern ecumenical agreement on mutual recognition of baptism rests on it. Cyprian, whose opinion the church rejected, is venerated as a saint and martyr by the church that rejected it, and Augustine made a point of saying so. The East never wholly settled the matter: Basil's first canonical letter distinguishes heresy from schism from unlawful assembly and allows the local bishops discretion; canon 95 in Trullo sorts converts by group; the decree of the three patriarchs in 1755 required the rebaptism of Latin converts; and Orthodox practice today ranges from chrismation to full baptism depending on the jurisdiction — an untidiness that Orthodox canonists defend as economy rather than inconsistency.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Cyprian, Epistulae 69–75 — English in G. W. Clarke, The Letters of St Cyprian of Carthage, 4 vols (Ancient Christian Writers)",note:"The dossier of the quarrel itself, including Ep. 75 from Firmilian, the fiercest third-century attack on Roman claims to survive."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Sententiae episcoporum numero LXXXVII de haereticis baptizandis (Carthage, 1 September 256), printed among Cyprian's works",note:"The eighty-seven individual sentences with the preface disclaiming any bishop of bishops — the text the entry's remark about tyrannical terror comes from."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Cyprian, De ecclesiae catholicae unitate",note:"Where the ecclesiology that makes rebaptism a necessary conclusion is set out; the two recensions of its fourth chapter are themselves a standing textual dispute."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Augustine, De baptismo contra Donatistas",note:"The theory Stephen's practice had lacked — validity distinguished from fruitfulness — argued while insisting throughout on Cyprian's sanctity."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Basil of Caesarea, Ep. 188 (First Canonical Letter), canon 1; Council in Trullo, canon 95 — English in Henry R. Percival, The Seven Ecumenical Councils (NPNF, second series, vol. 14)",note:"The Eastern settlement, which sorts converts by group and leaves the local bishops discretion rather than laying down one rule."},{kind:"primary",ref:"The Oros of 1755 issued by Cyril V of Constantinople with the patriarchs of Alexandria and Jerusalem — printed in Nicodemus the Hagiorite, The Rudder (Pedalion)",note:"The decree requiring the rebaptism of Latin converts, which is why Orthodox practice still varies from chrismation to baptism by jurisdiction."},{kind:"study",ref:"J. Patout Burns, Cyprian the Bishop (London, 2002)",note:"The clearest account of why Cyprian's position follows from his doctrine of the church rather than from rigorism about purity."},{kind:"study",ref:"Geoffrey D. Dunn, Cyprian and the Bishops of Rome (Strathfield, 2007)",note:"Weighs how far the exchange with Stephen was really about Roman authority, and is sceptical of the maximal readings offered in both directions."}]},{slug:"donatism",name:"Donatism",span:"311 – c. 700",parties:"The party its opponents named Donatists, after Donatus, and which called itself simply the church of the martyrs, against the Caecilianist or Catholic party, in Roman Africa — the two communions being of roughly equal size for a century.",question:"Does the validity of the sacraments depend on the sanctity of the minister, and can a church that compromised under persecution still be the church?",course:["The Great Persecution had required the surrender of the scriptures, and those who complied were called traditores, handers-over. When Caecilian was made bishop of Carthage in 311 by a consecrator alleged to have been one, a large part of the African church refused him and elected another, then Donatus. Their case was not that they were purer men but that a church which had absorbed apostasy into its own orders had ceased to be a church, and that the martyrs whose blood was still fresh in the province had died for something.","Constantine, newly a patron, referred the case to a Roman synod in 313 and then to the Council of Arles in 314, which ruled against them and laid down that ordination by a traditor holds good. They appealed to the emperor and lost; they were coerced, then tolerated, then coerced again, and they endured all of it, which confirmed them in the conviction that persecution is the mark of the true church.","Augustine took up the argument from 391 and made three moves that have been in Western theology ever since. The church in this world is a mixed body, wheat and tares together until the harvest, and any attempt to separate them early is both presumptuous and futile. The sacrament belongs to Christ and not to the minister, so that their baptisms are valid, which is precisely why they need not be repeated. And catholicity is a fact about extension: the whole world judges securely, he wrote, and a communion confined to one African province, however righteous, has by that confinement answered the question of who has left whom. Fourteen centuries later that sentence, read in a magazine article, broke John Henry Newman's confidence in the Church of England, as he described in the Apologia.","At the Conference of Carthage in June 411, convened by the imperial commissioner Marcellinus, two hundred and eighty-six Catholic and two hundred and seventy-nine Donatist bishops appeared — the fairest measure we have of how evenly the province was divided. Marcellinus ruled for the Catholics and proscription followed. It is here that Augustine's darkest legacy begins: having earlier opposed compulsion in religion, he came to defend it, reading the compel them to come in of the parable as warrant for imperial coercion of schismatics, while continuing to oppose the death penalty for them. That reading was quoted for a thousand years by people doing far worse things than Augustine contemplated."],outcome:"The movement outlasted its suppression, surviving in the African countryside until the Arab conquest removed the whole question. Whether it was also a movement of Berber and rural resistance to a Romanised urban church — the reading advanced by W. H. C. Frend in 1952 — has been sharply contested since, notably by Brent Shaw, who reads the violence as intra-Christian rather than ethnic or economic; the debate is live. What is certain is that the question did not die with the movement. The Novatianists had asked it before, the medieval Cathars and the Anabaptists and every gathered church of the pure since have asked it again, and any congregation that has ever wondered whether it could receive communion from a minister it despises is asking it now. Augustine's answer — that the church is a hospital and not a museum of the healed, and that the sacrament does not depend on the hand that gives it — is the majority answer, and it has always been the more consoling and the less obviously scriptural of the two.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Optatus of Milevis, Against the Donatists, trans. Mark Edwards (Translated Texts for Historians, Liverpool, 1997)",note:"The earliest Catholic case, written a generation before Augustine, and its appendix preserves the documents of the proceedings of 311–314."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Augustine, Contra epistulam Parmeniani; De baptismo; Epp. 93 and 185 (De correctione Donatistarum)",note:"The three moves the entry describes — mixed body, sacrament independent of the minister, catholicity as extension — and, in the letters, the defence of coercion in his own words."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Gesta Collationis Carthaginiensis anno 411, ed. Serge Lancel (Sources Chrétiennes; also CCSL 149A)",note:"The stenographic record of the 411 conference, and the source of the bishop-counts that measure how evenly the province was divided."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Maureen A. Tilley, Donatist Martyr Stories: The Church in Conflict in Roman North Africa (Translated Texts for Historians, Liverpool, 1996)",note:"The movement's own texts in translation — the nearest thing to hearing the church of the martyrs describe itself rather than be described."},{kind:"primary",ref:"John Henry Newman, Apologia Pro Vita Sua",note:"For the effect of Augustine's sentence about the whole world judging securely on a nineteenth-century Anglican, which is the entry's claim about the argument's afterlife."},{kind:"study",ref:"W. H. C. Frend, The Donatist Church: A Movement of Protest in Roman North Africa (Oxford, 1952)",note:"The social and ethnic reading of the schism stated at its strongest; it is now chiefly the position other historians argue against."},{kind:"study",ref:"Brent D. Shaw, Sacred Violence: African Christians and Sectarian Hatred in the Age of Augustine (Cambridge, 2011)",note:"The principal modern answer to Frend, reading the violence as intra-Christian rather than Berber, rural or economic."}]},{slug:"post-nicene-creeds",name:"The Creeds Between the Councils, 325 to 381",span:"325 – 381",parties:"The Nicene party under Athanasius and later the Cappadocians; the Homoians, who confessed the Son like the Father according to the scriptures; the Homoiousians, who confessed him like in substance; the Heteroousians under Aetius and Eunomius, whom their opponents called Anomoeans, unlike-ers, and who held that the Son is unlike the Father in substance; and the emperors, who mostly wanted quiet.",question:"If ὁμοούσιος is unscriptural, has a contested past and can be read modalistically, what word will say what Nicaea meant without saying more than it meant?",course:["The objection to Nicaea's keyword was not merely political. The term had been rejected at Antioch in 268 in connection with the case of Paul of Samosata — exactly who used it there and in what sense is no longer recoverable, Athanasius's explanation being itself a later reconstruction; it appears in Valentinian sources; and to say Father and Son are of one substance can be heard to say that they are one thing under two names, which was the error of Sabellius and, as many Easterners believed, of Marcellus of Ancyra, a signatory of Nicaea in good standing. Half the bishops of the East spent forty years looking for a formula that would exclude Arius without importing Sabellius, and they were not fools or crypto-heretics for doing so.","The creeds accumulated: the Dedication Creed at Antioch in 341; the collapse of Serdica in 343, where the Eastern and Western bishops met in separate buildings and excommunicated each other; the Long-lined Creed of 344; and then the run of Sirmian formulae, of which the second, in 357, forbade the use of the word substance altogether on the ground that it is not in scripture and is beyond human knowledge — Hilary of Poitiers called it the blasphemy. The Dated Creed of 359 was imposed at the twin councils of Ariminum and Seleucia and confirmed at Constantinople in 360, giving the empire an official Homoian faith; whence Jerome's celebrated line that the whole world groaned and marvelled to find itself Arian.","The way out was terminological and it was Athanasius who opened it. At Alexandria in 362 he accepted that those who spoke of three hypostases and those who spoke of one might mean the same thing, and should be judged by their meaning. The Cappadocians then made the distinction permanent: one substance and three hypostases, the common and the proper, the three distinguished not by rank or by degree of divinity but only by their relations of origin — unbegottenness, begottenness and procession. Basil argued the Spirit's divinity from the church's own doxology without ever calling him God in so many words, an economy his friend Gregory of Nazianzus criticised at the time and then abandoned in his fifth theological oration, where he says it plainly.","Modern scholarship has substantially dismantled the older picture. R. P. C. Hanson's Search for the Christian Doctrine of God and Lewis Ayres's Nicaea and its Legacy argue that there was no Arian party, no Arian church and little Arian influence — that 'Arianism' is largely a polemical construction of Athanasius, used to tar a wide range of theologies with the name of a presbyter most of them had never followed. The traditional narrative of one heresy resisted by one hero is not what the sources support, and the point is worth making because the traditional narrative is still the one most Christians are taught."],outcome:"381, and the creed we still sing. But the Homoian faith did not die with the empire's change of policy: Ulfilas, consecrated in 341, had carried it to the Goths with a Gothic Bible, and it became the faith of the Visigoths, Vandals, Ostrogoths, Burgundians and Lombards. For two centuries the Germanic west was Homoian and the Roman population Nicene, living side by side in the same cities under separate hierarchies. The Visigoths converted at the Third Council of Toledo in 589 — the council at which, to underline the Son's equality with the Father against the faith they were abandoning, the West first recited the creed with the filioque, and so began the next quarrel but one.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Athanasius, De synodis; Tomus ad Antiochenos",note:"The creeds of Ariminum and Seleucia quoted in full by an opponent, and the 362 concession that one hypostasis and three may be meant in the same sense."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Hilary of Poitiers, De synodis; Liber contra Constantium",note:"The Latin report on the Eastern formulae, and the source of the word blasphemia for the second Sirmian creed of 357."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Jerome, Dialogus adversus Luciferianos 19",note:"Where the line about the whole world groaning to find itself Arian is actually written — by a partisan, decades after the events it summarises."},{kind:"lexicon",ref:"August Hahn, Bibliothek der Symbole und Glaubensregeln der alten Kirche, 3rd ed. (Breslau, 1897)",note:"The standard collection in which the whole run of fourth-century creeds can be read one after another in the original."},{kind:"lexicon",ref:"J. N. D. Kelly, Early Christian Creeds, 3rd ed. (1972)",note:"The English guide through the same material, and the account of what each successive formula was framed to exclude."},{kind:"study",ref:"R. P. C. Hanson, The Search for the Christian Doctrine of God: The Arian Controversy 318–381 (Edinburgh, 1988)",note:"The book behind the entry's claim that there was no Arian party or church for these creeds to have been aimed at."},{kind:"study",ref:"Lewis Ayres, Nicaea and its Legacy (Oxford, 2004)",note:"Treats the forty-year search for a formula as a genuine theological problem rather than as evasion by crypto-heretics."},{kind:"study",ref:"Michel René Barnes and Daniel H. Williams (eds.), Arianism after Arius (Edinburgh, 1993)",note:"Essays taking the Homoian and Heteroousian theologies seriously as positions with their own logic and their own scriptural case."},{kind:"primary",ref:"The acts of the Third Council of Toledo (589), in José Vives (ed.), Concilios visigóticos e hispano-romanos (Barcelona and Madrid, 1963)",note:"The Visigothic abandonment of the Homoian faith, and the first recitation of the creed with the filioque, in the council's own record."},{kind:"study",ref:"E. A. Thompson, The Visigoths in the Time of Ulfila (Oxford, 1966)",note:"How the Homoian confession became Germanic, which is why the creed that lost in the empire outlived the empire that abandoned it."}]},{slug:"origenist",name:"The Origenist Controversies",span:"c. 393 – 404, and c. 530 – 553",parties:"In the first round, Epiphanius of Salamis, Jerome and Theophilus of Alexandria against Rufinus of Aquileia, John of Jerusalem and the monks of Nitria, with John Chrysostom destroyed in the crossfire. In the second, the rival monasteries of the Judaean desert, with Justinian legislating.",question:"Was the greatest exegete of the early church a heretic — and can his speculations be separated from his method?",course:["What Origen taught should be stated as he taught it. In the Peri Archon, written in the 220s and explicitly offered as inquiry where the church's rule of faith is silent, he proposed that rational creatures were made before bodies and equal, that they fell away by a cooling of love, that the material world is the arena of their correction rather than a prison, and that God's purpose may at last restore all rational beings, perhaps the devil included — a hope he marks repeatedly as speculation and elsewhere denies holding as doctrine. He was also the first systematic textual critic of the Bible, the compiler of the Hexapla, the theorist of the spiritual senses of scripture, a man who wrote commentary on nearly every book of the Bible, and a confessor tortured under Decius who died of it around 253.","The first round began when Epiphanius, who had catalogued Origen's errors in the Panarion, preached against him in Jerusalem in 393 in the presence of a bishop who admired him. Jerome broke with his oldest friend Rufinus over Rufinus's Latin version of the Peri Archon, which had softened the difficult passages on the plea that heretics had corrupted the text; Jerome answered with a literal translation designed to expose it, and the two spent years abusing each other in print. Theophilus of Alexandria, an Origenist until it became inconvenient, expelled the Origenist monks from Nitria, pursued them to Constantinople where Chrysostom sheltered them, and used the affair to depose Chrysostom at the Synod of the Oak in 403. Origenism was the pretext; the destruction of Chrysostom was the point.","The second round was fought among the Palestinian monks between the New Laura and the Great Laura, the parties nicknamed Isochristoi and Protoktistoi — those who held the risen souls would become equal to Christ, and those who held Christ's soul alone was first-created. Justinian issued an edict against Origen, with a list of anathemas appended, in a letter to the Patriarch Menas in 543, and fifteen anathemas against the Origenists were promulgated in connection with the council of 553. Whether the Fifth Council itself passed them, or a preceding synod did and they were attached to the council's records afterwards, is genuinely disputed among specialists, and the doctrines they condemn are in several cases the sixth-century Origenism of Evagrius and Stephen Bar Sudaili rather than anything Origen wrote."],outcome:"The man was condemned and the method was kept. Latin and Greek exegesis remained Origenist in its bones — the fourfold sense, the reading of the whole Bible as one book about Christ — and the anthology of his work made by Basil and Gregory of Nazianzus survived under their names. Evagrius Ponticus, condemned with him, went underground and came out in Syriac and under the name of Nilus, and his scheme of the eight thoughts became, through Cassian and Gregory the Great, the seven deadly sins. The universalist question was never closed: Gregory of Nyssa taught something very like the restoration of all and was never condemned; the Syriac tradition preserved a strand of it, and the second part of Isaac of Nineveh, which speaks of it with great tenderness, was unknown to scholarship until a manuscript was identified in the Bodleian in 1983 and published in 1995. The modern argument — Balthasar's hope that all may be saved, and the Orthodox and evangelical replies to it — is continuous with the ancient one, and the ancient one was never quite adjudicated.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Origen, On First Principles — Greek and Latin with French in Henri Crouzel and Manlio Simonetti (eds.), Traité des principes (Sources Chrétiennes); English trans. G. W. Butterworth",note:"The book itself, whose preface offers the speculations as inquiry where the rule of faith is silent — the whole of its author's defence."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Rufinus's Latin version of the Peri Archon, and Jerome, Ep. 124 (to Avitus)",note:"The two rival Latin renderings — one softened on the plea that heretics had corrupted the text, one literal and designed to convict — which is why the state of the text is itself the question."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Epiphanius, Panarion 64; Jerome, Contra Ioannem Hierosolymitanum; Rufinus, Apologia contra Hieronymum",note:"The first round in the words of the men who conducted it, including the catalogue of errors from which Epiphanius preached in Jerusalem."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Cyril of Scythopolis, Lives of the Monks of Palestine, trans. R. M. Price (Kalamazoo, 1991)",note:"The near-contemporary narrative of the Judaean desert monasteries and effectively the only source for the Isochristoi and Protoktistoi."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Justinian, Letter to the Patriarch Menas (543), and the fifteen anathemas against the Origenists — texts and dossier in Franz Diekamp, Die origenistischen Streitigkeiten im sechsten Jahrhundert und das fünfte allgemeine Concil (Münster, 1899)",note:"Both condemnations together, which is the only way to weigh the disputed question of whether 553 itself passed the fifteen or inherited them."},{kind:"study",ref:"Elizabeth A. Clark, The Origenist Controversy: The Cultural Construction of an Early Christian Debate (Princeton, 1992)",note:"The first round as a quarrel about asceticism, patronage and friendship in which doctrine was partly the instrument — the destruction of Chrysostom included."},{kind:"study",ref:"Daniël Hombergen, The Second Origenist Controversy (Studia Anselmiana, Rome, 2001)",note:"Argues that sixth-century Origenism is Evagrian rather than Origen's, which is the ground of the entry's claim about what the anathemas actually condemn."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Isaac of Nineveh, The Second Part, ed. and trans. Sebastian P. Brock (CSCO, Louvain, 1995)",note:"The text unknown to scholarship until a Bodleian manuscript was identified in 1983 — the Syriac survival of the universalist hope in its gentlest form."},{kind:"study",ref:"Hans Urs von Balthasar, Dare We Hope 'That All Men Be Saved'?",note:"The modern phase of the one question the ancient controversy never adjudicated, and the book its Orthodox and evangelical critics are answering."}]},{slug:"pelagius-augustine",name:"Pelagius against Augustine",span:"411 – 431, and 529",parties:"Pelagius, a British ascetic teaching in Rome, with Caelestius and later Julian of Eclanum, against Augustine of Hippo, Jerome, the African councils and eventually Rome.",question:"Can a human being keep God's commandments — and if not, why does God command them? What does baptism do for an infant?",course:["Pelagius was a moral reformer before he was anything else, appalled by the laxity of a Christianity that had become respectable and by what he heard as fatalism in Augustine's prayer that God would give what he commands and command what he wills. His positions, as he stated them: God would not command what cannot be done, so the capacity to obey must exist; that capacity is itself God's gift in creation, though the willing and the doing are ours; sin spreads by imitation and habit rather than by inheritance; death is a condition of nature rather than a penalty; and infants are baptised not to remit a guilt they cannot have contracted but for sanctification and adoption into the kingdom. Caelestius stated all of this more provocatively. Julian of Eclanum, the ablest of them, defended the goodness of nature, of the body and of marriage, and charged that Augustine's doctrine of inherited guilt was Manichaeism that had never been renounced — a charge some modern historians think worth taking seriously.","Augustine's counter-case was that the whole race is implicated in Adam, on his reading of Romans 5:12, where the Latin in quo, in whom, translates a Greek phrase that most modern exegetes render because — a translation crux with as large a consequence as any in the history of Christian doctrine. From it he built: the will is free and is not free for the good without grace; grace precedes, prepares and accompanies every good act; election is gratuitous and the number of the elect is God's business; and the mass of humanity is justly condemned, so that mercy to any is sheer gift.","The process ran a decade and twice reversed. Caelestius was condemned at Carthage in 411; Pelagius was acquitted at Diospolis in Palestine in 415, before Greek bishops who found nothing objectionable in what he said and who did not share the Western frame of the question; the African councils of 416 condemned both; Innocent I concurred; his successor Zosimus in 417 declared Pelagius orthodox after reading his defence; and after a further African council and an imperial rescript, Zosimus reversed himself in the Epistula tractoria of 418. Eighteen Italian bishops who refused to subscribe, Julian among them, went into exile. Ephesus in 431 endorsed the condemnation, which is why this quarrel touches the conciliar list at all.","The sequel was fought in southern Gaul, and the label semi-Pelagian that the textbooks give it is a coinage of the sixteenth century that no one involved would have accepted. John Cassian, Vincent of Lérins and Faustus of Riez granted the necessity of grace throughout and questioned only unconditional predestination and whether the first movement of faith might be the creature's; Prosper of Aquitaine answered them for Augustine. The Second Council of Orange settled it in 529 under Caesarius of Arles: grace precedes every merit and the beginning of faith itself is God's gift, while anyone who says God predestines anyone to evil is anathema. Boniface II confirmed it in 531, and then Orange was almost completely forgotten until the sixteenth century, when its rediscovery embarrassed both sides of a debate that had been proceeding without it."],outcome:"The West received Augustine and has argued about how much of him to keep ever since; every subsequent Western quarrel about grace, from Gottschalk to Dort to the Jansenists, is a continuation. The East never had this argument in these terms and does not use them: Greek theology speaks of ancestral sin as an inherited mortality and corruption rather than an inherited guilt, and of synergy, the cooperation of a will that grace heals rather than replaces. That is not a small difference of emphasis but the deepest structural divergence between Eastern and Western accounts of salvation, and it is the reason that Orthodox observers have generally found the Reformation debate about justification to be a family argument in a language they do not speak.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Pelagius, Commentary on Romans, trans. Theodore de Bruyn (Oxford Early Christian Studies, 1993); Letter to Demetrias",note:"Pelagius in his own words rather than in his opponents' excerpts, which is where the motive of moral reform becomes visible."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Augustine, De peccatorum meritis et remissione; De spiritu et littera; De natura et gratia; Contra Iulianum and the Opus imperfectum contra Iulianum",note:"The case built from Romans 5:12 in the Latin in quo, and, in the works against Julian, the Manichaean charge made and answered at length."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Augustine, Confessions X.29.40",note:"The prayer that God give what he commands and command what he wills, in its original setting — the sentence Pelagius is reported to have found intolerable."},{kind:"lexicon",ref:"Denzinger, Enchiridion Symbolorum",note:"The canons of Carthage 418, the Indiculus on grace and the canons of Orange in the numbering by which all later argument cites them."},{kind:"primary",ref:"The canons of the Second Council of Orange (529), in Charles de Clercq (ed.), Concilia Galliae A. 511–A. 695 (CCSL 148A)",note:"The settlement of the Gallic sequel, including the explicit anathema on predestination to evil that the sixteenth century rediscovered too late."},{kind:"study",ref:"Rebecca Harden Weaver, Divine Grace and Human Agency: A Study of the Semi-Pelagian Controversy (Macon, 1996)",note:"Treats Cassian, Vincent and Faustus as making a serious argument, and dates the label the textbooks apply to them."},{kind:"study",ref:"Ali Bonner, The Myth of Pelagianism (Oxford, 2018)",note:"The strongest recent statement of the case that the coherent heresy called Pelagianism is a construction of its opponents."},{kind:"study",ref:"Peter Brown, Augustine of Hippo: A Biography, rev. ed. (Berkeley, 2000)",note:"Why the controversy took the shape it did in Augustine's own life and in the politics of the African church."},{kind:"study",ref:"John Meyendorff, Byzantine Theology: Historical Trends and Doctrinal Themes (New York, 1974)",note:"The Greek reading of ἐφ᾽ ᾧ at Romans 5:12 and of ancestral sin as inherited mortality — the divergence the entry calls structural rather than one of emphasis."}]},{slug:"three-chapters",name:"The Three Chapters",span:"544 – 698",parties:"Justinian and the Eastern episcopate against the Latin West, above all Africa and the churches of northern Italy; Pope Vigilius in the middle and crushed by it.",question:"May the church condemn men who died in its communion — and does condemning them overturn the council that received them?",course:["Justinian's purpose was reunion. If the miaphysites of Egypt and Syria could be shown that Chalcedon was Cyril's council and not a covert rehabilitation of Antioch, the empire's eastern provinces might come back. The instrument he chose was the condemnation of three items: the person and writings of Theodore of Mopsuestia, dead in the church's peace in 428 and the master of the whole Antiochene exegetical school; the writings of Theodoret of Cyrrhus against Cyril's anathemas; and the letter of Ibas of Edessa to Mari the Persian, which spoke slightingly of Cyril.","The Western objection was substantive and procedural at once, and both halves were strong. Substantively, Chalcedon had restored Theodoret and Ibas to their sees, and the commissioners at Chalcedon had heard Ibas's letter read; to anathematise now what the council had then received was to unpick the council. Procedurally, to condemn a man who died in communion is to pass sentence on someone who can neither answer nor repent, which African canon law regarded as simply impermissible. Facundus of Hermiane put the case in twelve books, and it has never been well answered.","Vigilius was fetched to Constantinople, arriving in 547, and never went home. He condemned the Chapters in a Iudicatum of 548, withdrew it when the African church excommunicated him for it, refused again in a Constitutum of May 553 — his name was struck from the diptychs in reply — and capitulated in December, confirming the council he had boycotted. He died at Syracuse on the journey back in 555. Milan and Aquileia broke communion; the bishop of Aquileia took the title of patriarch, and the Istrian schism ran for a century and a half, ending at a synod at Pavia around 698."],outcome:"It failed at what it was for. Egypt and Syria did not return, and within eighty years they had passed out of the empire altogether and the question became academic. The cost was enormous: a pope publicly coerced into reversing a solemn judgement, a schism in Italy lasting a hundred and fifty years, and a permanent grievance for the Church of the East, whose chief doctor Theodore had now been anathematised by the imperial church. Yet what the council achieved theologically was necessary. Without the clarification that the single hypostasis of Christ is the pre-existent Word himself, Chalcedon's definition can be read as leaving two subjects side by side; the neo-Chalcedonian synthesis is what makes the Definition mean what its defenders had always said it meant, and every subsequent reading of Chalcedon, including the modern agreed statements with the Oriental Orthodox, depends on it.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Richard Price, The Acts of the Council of Constantinople of 553, 2 vols (Translated Texts for Historians, Liverpool, 2009)",note:"The acts in English with Vigilius's documents attached, and an introduction that weighs how far the pope's final consent was coerced."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Facundus of Hermiane, Pro defensione trium capitulorum (CCSL 90A; also in Sources Chrétiennes)",note:"The Western case in twelve books; the entry's claim that it has never been well answered is a claim about this text specifically."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Vigilius, Iudicatum (surviving fragments) and the Constitutum of 14 May 553 — in Otto Günther (ed.), Collectio Avellana (CSEL 35)",note:"The pope's successive positions in their own wording, which is the only way to measure how complete the December capitulation was."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Justinian, On the Right Faith (the edict on the Three Chapters) — English in K. P. Wesche, On the Person of Christ: The Christology of Emperor Justinian (Crestwood, 1991)",note:"The emperor's theology stated as theology rather than as policy, which is how the neo-Chalcedonian argument was actually made."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Paul the Deacon, Historia Langobardorum",note:"The Lombard narrative of the Istrian schism and of the synod at Pavia that ended it — nearly the only source for the schism's last century."},{kind:"study",ref:"Celia Chazelle and Catherine Cubitt (eds.), The Crisis of the Oikoumene: The Three Chapters and the Failed Quest for Unity in the Sixth-Century Mediterranean (Turnhout, 2007)",note:"The Western refusal treated as its own history rather than as obstruction of a settlement already reached in the East."},{kind:"study",ref:"Patrick T. R. Gray, The Defense of Chalcedon in the East (451–553) (Leiden, 1979)",note:"Why the clarification that the single hypostasis is the pre-existent Word was necessary for Chalcedon to mean what its defenders had said it meant."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Agreed Statements of the Joint Commission for Theological Dialogue between the Orthodox Church and the Oriental Orthodox Churches (Anba Bishoy, 1989; Chambésy, 1990)",note:"The modern reading of Chalcedon that the neo-Chalcedonian clarification made possible — the reunion 553 was for, arriving fourteen centuries late."}]},{slug:"iconoclasm",name:"Iconoclasm, in Two Rounds",span:"726 – 787 and 815 – 843",parties:"The emperors Leo III, Constantine V, Leo V and Theophilos with the bishops who supported them — iconoclast, image-breaker, and the Greek εἰκονομάχοι, image-fighters, are both their opponents' names for them; they called themselves orthodox — against the monks, John of Damascus writing safely outside the empire under Muslim rule, the Patriarch Nikephoros, Theodore the Stoudite, and two empresses.",question:"Is a painted image of Christ an idol, a falsehood about his person, or a confession of the incarnation?",course:["The iconoclast case had three legs and only the first is the obvious one. There is the second commandment and the charge of idolatry — with the abuses the sources report, of panels standing as sponsors at baptism and of paint scraped from an icon into the chalice. There is the argument from the church's own austerity in its early centuries. And there is a christological argument of real force, set out at the Council of Hieria in 754 and in Constantine V's own Peuseis: a portrait circumscribes its subject; the divinity cannot be circumscribed; so an image of Christ either takes in the divinity, which confuses the natures as Eutyches did, or depicts the humanity by itself, which divides the person as Nestorius did; therefore the only image of Christ he himself gave us is the eucharist, which is his body without being a likeness of it.","The answer had to be as technical as the objection. John of Damascus argued that the invisible God has himself become visible and therefore depictable, and that to refuse the image is to be embarrassed by the incarnation; he added the distinction between reverence, which may be given relatively to persons and things and passes to what they represent, and worship, which is God's alone. Theodore the Stoudite and Nikephoros supplied the missing precision: an image is a likeness of a hypostasis, not of a nature. It depicts the person of the Word as he is in his flesh, and no more circumscribes his divinity than his mother's arms did.","The historian's difficulty is that almost every source is iconodule. The iconoclast writings survive chiefly as quotations inside the refutations of their opponents, which means we read Constantine V's theology through Nikephoros's excerpts. How much of the first round was really about images and how much about the emperor's control of monastic land and manpower is argued and cannot be settled on the evidence we have. What is clear is that the second round, from 815, was a deliberate imperial revival rather than a spontaneous movement, and that it ended with the restoration of the icons on the first Sunday of Lent in 843 under the Empress Theodora — kept ever since as the Triumph of Orthodoxy, with the Synodikon read from the ambo.","The West ran a separate and largely confused track. Charlemagne's court received a Latin version of Nicaea II that rendered both Greek words for reverence and worship as adoratio, and so read the Greeks as prescribing the worship of pictures; the Opus Caroli regis attacked the council on that basis and the Council of Frankfurt in 794 condemned what it had been told the Greeks said. Rome held to the council. The misreading was eventually corrected, but the West never acquired the East's positive theology of the image, and images in Western churches remained a pedagogical convenience rather than a dogmatic statement."],outcome:"The East gained not a permission but a doctrine: after canon 82 in Trullo required that Christ be shown as a man and not as a lamb, and Nicaea II defined the veneration, the icon became a confession of faith, and Orthodox worship is now unimaginable without it. The West, having kept its images without a theology of them, lost a great many of them very quickly in the sixteenth century. The Reformed churches teach today, from the second commandment and the principle that God is to be worshipped only as he has commanded, that images are not to be used in worship — the Heidelberg Catechism putting it that God will have his people taught by the living preaching of his word and not by dumb idols. That is a considered scriptural conviction held by tens of millions of Christians, and it is the iconoclast question still being asked, though from a different premise and with no imperial soldiers behind it.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Richard Price, The Acts of the Second Council of Nicaea (787), 2 vols (Translated Texts for Historians, Liverpool, 2018)",note:"Where the Definition of Hieria survives, quoted clause by clause inside its own refutation — the christological argument of 754 in the fullest form we have it."},{kind:"primary",ref:"John of Damascus, Three Treatises on the Divine Images, trans. Andrew Louth (Crestwood, 2003); critical ed. Bonifatius Kotter, Die Schriften des Johannes von Damaskos III (Berlin, 1975)",note:"The reply written from outside the empire, with the distinction of relative reverence from worship on which everything afterwards rests."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Theodore the Stoudite, On the Holy Icons, trans. Catharine P. Roth; Nikephoros, Refutatio et eversio, ed. J. M. Featherstone (CCSG 33)",note:"The technical answer the entry turns on — that an image is the likeness of a hypostasis and not of a nature."},{kind:"lexicon",ref:"Leslie Brubaker and John Haldon, Byzantium in the Iconoclast Era (ca. 680–850): The Sources — An Annotated Survey (Aldershot, 2001)",note:"Establishes what actually survives and from whose hand, which is the ground of the entry's warning that almost every source is iconodule."},{kind:"study",ref:"Leslie Brubaker and John Haldon, Byzantium in the Iconoclast Era c. 680–850: A History (Cambridge, 2011)",note:"The revisionist history arguing that the first round was narrower, later and less concerned with images than the iconodule accounts allow."},{kind:"study",ref:"Marie-France Auzépy, L'histoire des iconoclastes (Paris, 2007)",note:"On how the iconodule victory rewrote the record, and what can still be reconstructed of Constantine V's own theology."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Ann Freeman (ed.), Opus Caroli regis contra synodum (the Libri Carolini), MGH Concilia II, Supplementum I",note:"The Frankish attack on a council its authors had read in a translation that rendered both Greek words as adoratio."},{kind:"lexicon",ref:"Jean Gouillard, 'Le Synodikon de l'Orthodoxie: édition et commentaire,' Travaux et Mémoires 2 (1967)",note:"The critical edition of the text proclaimed on the first Sunday of Lent — what 843 actually left behind as a permanent liturgical act."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Heidelberg Catechism, Q&A 96–98; Westminster Larger Catechism, Q. 109",note:"The Reformed position stated confessionally: the same question asked from the second commandment and the regulative principle rather than inherited from Byzantium."}]},{slug:"filioque",name:"The Filioque",span:"589 – present",parties:"The Latin West against the Greek East, with almost everyone on both sides for most of the time arguing two different questions at once.",question:"Does the Holy Spirit proceed from the Father alone, or from the Father and the Son — and quite separately, may anyone add a word to the creed of an ecumenical council?",course:["The two questions must be kept apart, and the failure to keep them apart is most of the history. The dogmatic question is whether the Son has a part in the Spirit's eternal origin. The canonical question is whether one part of the church may unilaterally alter a text that a council forbade anyone to alter — Ephesus having barred the composition of another creed, and the council of 879 having barred addition and subtraction outright. A Greek may in principle be satisfied on the first and unmoved on the second.","The clause was not a Roman invention. Augustine's trinitarian theology makes the Spirit the mutual gift and love of Father and Son, proceeding from the Father principally and from the Son as given by the Father; and the word entered a creed at the Third Council of Toledo in 589, when the Visigoths abandoned the Homoian faith and their bishops wished to hammer home the Son's full equality. It travelled north with the Franks and was sung in Charlemagne's chapel. When Frankish and Greek monks came to blows over it in Jerusalem in 807, Leo III backed the doctrine, refused the interpolation, and had the creed without it engraved on two silver shields set up in St Peter's. Rome itself adopted the clause only around 1014, at the coronation of Henry II.","Photius wrote the classic Eastern case in the Mystagogy: if the Spirit proceeds from the Son as well, either there are two causes in the Godhead, which destroys the monarchy of the Father on which the unity of the Trinity rests, or the Father and Son are collapsed into a single principle, which destroys their distinction. The Latin reply, that Father and Son are one principle in respect of spiration, is exactly what Photius anticipated and rejected. But the East had its own resources for saying something like what the Latins meant: Cyril and Maximus both use 'through the Son', and Maximus's letter to Marinus, explaining to Greek readers that the Latins do not make the Son a cause of the Spirit's being, is the single most quoted text in every modern attempt at rapprochement. Later Byzantine theology, in Gregory II of Cyprus and Gregory Palamas, distinguished the Spirit's eternal procession from the Father from his eternal manifestation through the Son, which is a genuine development and not a mere concession.","The modern movement has been considerable. In 1995 the Pontifical Council for Promoting Christian Unity published a clarification stating that the Father alone is the cause (αἰτία) of the Spirit, and that the Greek ἐκπόρευσις and the Latin processio are not exact equivalents — the Greek naming origination from the cause, the Latin naming a more general derivation — so that the Latin formula, correctly translated, does not assert what the Greeks deny. The North American Orthodox-Catholic Consultation recommended in 2003 that the Catholic Church cease using the clause in the creed liturgically. Lambeth Conferences in 1978 and 1988 recommended its removal, and Old Catholics and several Anglican provinces have removed it; popes have repeatedly recited the creed in Greek without it on ecumenical occasions."],outcome:"Unfinished, but narrowed. Many theologians on both sides now hold that the terminological difference, once the two verbs are distinguished, is not church-dividing, and that Maximus settled the substance in the seventh century. Many Orthodox hold that the canonical grievance is now the larger obstacle and that removing the clause from the Latin creed would cost Rome nothing and mean everything. And a substantial body of Orthodox theologians holds that the difference is real, that the Latin scheme subordinates the person of the Spirit to the relation between Father and Son, and that no amount of philology will dissolve it. All three positions are held by serious people in good standing, and this page does not adjudicate between them.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Augustine, De Trinitate, especially book XV",note:"Where the Spirit is said to proceed from the Father principaliter and from the Son as the Father's gift — the root of the Latin doctrine, and not of the interpolation."},{kind:"primary",ref:"The acts of the Third Council of Toledo (589), in José Vives (ed.), Concilios visigóticos e hispano-romanos (Barcelona and Madrid, 1963)",note:"The clause's first appearance in a recited creed, at a council converting Homoian Goths — the polemical context the entry insists on."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Photius, Mystagogy of the Holy Spirit (PG 102)",note:"The classic Eastern argument, and it is about causality and the monarchy of the Father rather than about Frankish presumption."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Maximus the Confessor, Letter to Marinus of Cyprus (PG 91)",note:"The seventh-century Greek explanation that the Latins do not make the Son a cause of the Spirit's being — the single most quoted text in every modern rapprochement."},{kind:"primary",ref:"The acts of the council of 879–880, in J. D. Mansi, Sacrorum Conciliorum Nova et Amplissima Collectio",note:"The decree barring any addition to or subtraction from the creed, which is the canonical half of the quarrel in its sharpest form."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Anselm, De processione Spiritus Sancti; the bull Laetentur caeli (Florence, 1439) in Norman P. Tanner (ed.), Decrees of the Ecumenical Councils, vol. 1",note:"The Latin case argued to Greeks, and the formula of union — one principle, one spiration — that Photius had refused in advance."},{kind:"study",ref:"A. Edward Siecienski, The Filioque: History of a Doctrinal Controversy (Oxford, 2010)",note:"The standard history from the New Testament to the present, and the place to check any claim made about this dispute in either direction."},{kind:"study",ref:"Aristeides Papadakis, Crisis in Byzantium: The Filioque Controversy in the Patriarchate of Gregory II of Cyprus (New York, 1983)",note:"For eternal manifestation through the Son as a genuine Byzantine development rather than a concession made under pressure."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Pontifical Council for Promoting Christian Unity, 'The Greek and Latin Traditions regarding the Procession of the Holy Spirit' (1995)",note:"The Roman clarification that the Father alone is αἰτία and that ἐκπόρευσις and processio are not equivalents — the text the entry summarises."},{kind:"primary",ref:"North American Orthodox–Catholic Theological Consultation, 'The Filioque: A Church-Dividing Issue?' (25 October 2003)",note:"The joint statement recommending that the Catholic Church cease using the clause liturgically, with both traditions' reasoning set out at length."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Lukas Vischer (ed.), Spirit of God, Spirit of Christ: Ecumenical Reflections on the Filioque Controversy (Faith and Order Paper 103, Geneva, 1981)",note:"The Klingenthal memorandum and the studies behind the Anglican, Old Catholic and other removals of the clause."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Joint International Commission for the Theological Dialogue between the Catholic Church and the Orthodox Church, the Ravenna document (2007)",note:"Bears on the canonical half of the question rather than the dogmatic one: what may be decided at the universal level, and by whom, without common consent."}]},{slug:"gottschalk",name:"Gottschalk and Double Predestination",span:"c. 840 – 870, and after",parties:"Gottschalk of Orbais, a Saxon given to a monastery as a child against his will, against Hrabanus Maurus of Mainz and Hincmar of Reims; with Ratramnus of Corbie, Prudentius of Troyes, Lupus of Ferrières and Florus of Lyons largely on Gottschalk's side, and John Scotus Eriugena recruited against him to disastrous effect.",question:"Does God predestine the reprobate to damnation as he predestines the elect to glory — and did Augustine say so?",course:["Gottschalk taught a twin predestination: God from eternity predestines the elect to life and the reprobate to death. He was careful, and his care is usually forgotten. He insisted that God predestines the wicked to punishment, not to sin; that God foreknows sin without causing it; and that the predestination to death is a predestination to the just penalty of freely committed evil. He read this out of Augustine's late anti-Pelagian works and out of Fulgentius of Ruspe, and on the question of what those authors said he was, by most modern assessments, substantially right.","He was condemned at Mainz in 848 under Hrabanus, and again at Quierzy in 849 under Hincmar, where he was flogged, made to burn his own book with his own hands, and imprisoned in the monastery of Hautvillers. He stayed there about twenty years, refusing the terms of communion offered him, writing, and died unreconciled and without the sacraments around 868. He also wrote in that prison some of the finest Latin verse of the Carolingian age.","The learned reaction was mostly against Hincmar. Ratramnus, Prudentius, Lupus and Florus all defended a doctrine of double predestination in some form, and the Council of Valence in 855 effectively vindicated it in its opening chapters, confessing a predestination of the elect to life and of the reprobate to death while denying any predestination to sin. Hincmar had his own council at Quierzy in 853 with four chapters the other way, and the whole business was fudged at Tusey in 860 without anything being decided.","Hincmar's worst move was to commission John Scotus Eriugena, the one original metaphysician in the Latin ninth century, whose treatise of 851 argued that in a simple God foreknowledge and predestination are one, that God cannot know or predestine evil since evil is a privation, and that hell is not a place God prepares but the sinner's own perverted will contemplating its loss. It was condemned at Valence in 855 in terms of some contempt — as trifling little questions, old wives' tales and Irish porridge — and it damaged Hincmar's cause more than anything Gottschalk wrote."],outcome:"Nothing was settled and nothing has been. The same question returns with Bradwardine against the Ockhamists in the fourteenth century, and in full at the Reformation: Calvin's dreadful decree, as he himself named it in the Institutes, using a word that means awe-inspiring rather than wicked; the Synod of Dort in 1618, condemning the Remonstrants and defining unconditional election; and the Arminian and Wesleyan reply, which is the majority position among Protestants today. In the Catholic world it returned as the controversy de auxiliis between 1582 and 1607, the Dominican Domingo Báñez's physical premotion against the Jesuit Luis de Molina's middle knowledge — God's knowledge of what any free creature would freely do in any circumstance, which lets grace be efficacious without being coercive. Rome examined it for nine years in the Congregatio de Auxiliis and in 1607 Paul V forbade either party to call the other heretical and declined to decide, which is either a failure of nerve or the wisest thing any authority has done with this question. Molinism is at present a flourishing position in analytic philosophy of religion, and Gottschalk, who died excommunicate for saying less, has no grave anyone can find.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Gottschalk of Orbais, Œuvres théologiques et grammaticales, ed. Cyrille Lambot (Louvain, 1945)",note:"The surviving works, recovered and edited only in the twentieth century, on which any judgement of what he actually taught now depends."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Victor Genke and Francis X. Gumerlock, Gottschalk and a Medieval Predestination Controversy: Texts Translated from the Latin (Milwaukee, 2010)",note:"The confessions and the principal replies in English, where the care of his distinction between predestining to punishment and predestining to sin becomes visible."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Hincmar of Reims, De praedestinatione Dei et libero arbitrio (PL 125); Ratramnus of Corbie, De praedestinatione Dei (PL 121)",note:"The prosecution and one of the learned defences, printed in the same collection and seldom read against each other since."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Liber de tribus epistolis (PL 121)",note:"The Lyons reply to Hincmar, printed among the works of Remigius of Lyons and now generally assigned to Florus — the authorship question these Carolingian texts routinely raise."},{kind:"primary",ref:"John Scotus Eriugena, De divina praedestinatione, ed. Goulven Madec (CCCM 50); English trans. Mary Brennan, Treatise on Divine Predestination (Notre Dame, 1998)",note:"The treatise Hincmar commissioned and then had to disown, arguing that a simple God can neither know nor predestine a privation."},{kind:"primary",ref:"The four chapters of Quierzy (853) and the canons of Valence (855) — in MGH Concilia III, ed. Wilfried Hartmann; numbered text in Denzinger",note:"Two councils deciding the same question in opposite directions within two years, which is the documentary reason nothing was settled."},{kind:"study",ref:"Matthew Bryan Gillis, Heresy and Dissent in the Carolingian Empire: The Case of Gottschalk of Orbais (Oxford, 2017)",note:"The current monograph, and its case that the oblate's refusal of the vows made for him was what the bishops were really punishing."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Calvin, Institutes III.21–24; the Canons of the Synod of Dort (1619)",note:"The same doctrine restated where it did carry churches with it, with the decretum horribile readable in its own context rather than as a slogan."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Luis de Molina, Concordia liberi arbitrii cum gratiae donis (1588); part IV trans. Alfred J. Freddoso, On Divine Foreknowledge (Ithaca, 1988)",note:"Middle knowledge as its author framed it — the proposal Rome examined for nine years and then declined either to condemn or to endorse."},{kind:"study",ref:"Robert J. Matava, Divine Causality and Human Free Choice: Domingo Báñez, Physical Premotion and the Controversy de Auxiliis Revisited (Leiden, 2016)",note:"The fullest recent account of the Báñez–Molina dispute and of precisely what Paul V's silence in 1607 left open."},{kind:"study",ref:"Thomas P. Flint, Divine Providence: The Molinist Account (Ithaca, 1998)",note:"The live modern phase: Molinism argued as analytic philosophy of religion, which is the entry's point about a quarrel that never closed."}]},{slug:"investiture",name:"The Investiture Contest",span:"1075 – 1122",parties:"Gregory VII and his successors against the Emperors Henry IV and Henry V; in England, Anselm against William Rufus and Henry I; and behind both, the reforming party against the whole Carolingian and Ottonian settlement of church and crown.",question:"Who makes a bishop — the king who hands him the ring and the staff along with the lands and the office, or the church?",course:["The problem was structural before it was ideological. A bishop in the eleventh century was a great landholder, a royal counsellor and often a commander; no king could be indifferent to who held the see, and the ceremony by which he handed over the ring and crozier expressed a real dependence. The reformers, formed by Cluny and by the moral campaign against simony and clerical marriage, saw in that ceremony the root of the corruption: the office of a bishop was being sold and granted by laymen, and the church was a department of the state.","In 1075 Gregory VII had entered in his register twenty-seven terse propositions, the Dictatus papae, among them that the Roman pontiff alone may use the imperial insignia, that he may depose emperors, that he may be judged by no one, and that the Roman church has never erred and never will. In the same year his Lenten synod forbade lay investiture outright. Henry IV replied from Worms in January 1076 with a letter addressed to Hildebrand, at present not pope but false monk, ordering him to descend. Gregory excommunicated and deposed him and released his subjects from their oaths of fealty. That last act, not the excommunication, is the revolutionary one: it made the papacy a political power in the strict sense.","The German princes took the opening. Henry crossed the Alps in winter and stood before the castle of Canossa in January 1077 in penitential dress for three days until Gregory absolved him — an absolution that was Henry's political masterstroke as much as Gregory's triumph, since it removed the princes' pretext without conceding anything. They elected an anti-king anyway. Gregory excommunicated Henry again in 1080; Henry set up an antipope and took Rome in 1084; Gregory was rescued by Robert Guiscard's Normans, who sacked the city so thoroughly that the Romans would not have him back, and he died at Salerno in 1085. His followers report his last words as a claim to have loved justice and hated iniquity, and therefore to die in exile; the saying may not be his.","The solution came from canonists rather than from armies. Ivo of Chartres and others distinguished the spiritual office, which only the church can give, from the temporalities attached to it, which the king may. The Concordat of Worms in 1122 made the distinction law: the emperor renounced investiture with ring and staff and conceded free canonical election, while retaining the right to be present at German elections and to invest the elect with the regalia by the touch of a sceptre — before consecration in Germany, after it in Italy and Burgundy. The First Lateran Council confirmed it in 1123, the first of the councils in the Catholic list to be held in the West and the point at which the numbered series becomes a Western one."],outcome:"Neither side got what it wanted and Western civilisation got something neither had intended: two jurisdictions in one society, each with its own law, courts and claim on the same people. Whether that division is the origin of the Western legal tradition and, at a great distance, of the secular state is argued — Harold Berman's Law and Revolution makes the strong case and it has been criticised as overstated — but that Europe thereafter could never quite be a single sacred order is not in doubt. The East had no such contest. There the relation of emperor and patriarch was conceived on the model of the two great gifts of God, priesthood and empire, working in concert — Justinian's Sixth Novel is the classic text, and symphonia the later shorthand for it — and while patriarchs did excommunicate emperors, none claimed the authority to depose one. That difference in the constitution of church and state is as deep a divergence between East and West as anything in the creeds, and it developed almost without anyone arguing about it.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Das Register Gregors VII., ed. Erich Caspar (MGH Epistolae selectae 2); English in H. E. J. Cowdrey, The Register of Pope Gregory VII 1073–1085 (Oxford, 2002)",note:"The Dictatus papae where it actually stands, entered in the register for 1075, together with the depositions and the release of Henry's subjects from their oaths."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Die Briefe Heinrichs IV., ed. Carl Erdmann (MGH); English in Theodor E. Mommsen and Karl F. Morrison, Imperial Lives and Letters of the Eleventh Century (New York, 1962)",note:"The letter from Worms addressed to Hildebrand as false monk, and the royal case for the settlement the reformers were dismantling."},{kind:"primary",ref:"The Concordat of Worms (1122), in MGH Constitutiones et acta publica imperatorum et regum I; English in Brian Tierney, The Crisis of Church and State 1050–1300 (Englewood Cliffs, 1964)",note:"The two brief instruments themselves, in which the difference between ring and staff and the touch of a sceptre carries the entire solution."},{kind:"primary",ref:"First Lateran Council, canons — Norman P. Tanner (ed.), Decrees of the Ecumenical Councils, vol. 1 (1990)",note:"The conciliar confirmation of the concordat, and the point at which the numbered Catholic list becomes a Western series."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Ivo of Chartres, Letters (PL 162)",note:"The canonist's distinction between the spiritual office and the temporalities, worked out in correspondence years before it became a treaty."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Justinian, Novel 6, preface",note:"The classic statement of priesthood and empire as two gifts of God working in concert — the Eastern constitution against which the Western contest is measured."},{kind:"study",ref:"Uta-Renate Blumenthal, The Investiture Controversy: Church and Monarchy from the Ninth to the Twelfth Century (Philadelphia, 1988)",note:"The standard narrative, and clear that the problem was structural — bishops as landholders and counsellors — before it was ideological."},{kind:"study",ref:"H. E. J. Cowdrey, Pope Gregory VII, 1073–1085 (Oxford, 1998)",note:"The biography, and sceptical about both the traditional reading of Canossa and the authenticity of Gregory's reported last words."},{kind:"study",ref:"Harold J. Berman, Law and Revolution: The Formation of the Western Legal Tradition (Cambridge, Mass., 1983)",note:"The strong claim that the Western legal tradition begins with this contest — the thesis the entry reports as argued and as criticised for overstatement."},{kind:"study",ref:"Gilbert Dagron, Emperor and Priest: The Imperial Office in Byzantium (Cambridge, 2003)",note:"The Byzantine side of the comparison, and why no patriarch ever claimed the authority to depose an emperor."}]},{slug:"justification",name:"Justification at the Reformation",span:"1515 – 1999",parties:"Luther and the reformers against Rome, and thereafter the confessions against one another and against themselves.",question:"Is a sinner justified by faith alone, God reckoning to him a righteousness that is Christ's, or by a grace that makes him actually righteous, faith working through love?",course:["Luther's question was pastoral before it was academic. What does Paul mean by the righteousness of God in Romans 1:17 — the righteousness by which God is just and punishes the unjust, which is how Luther says he had understood and hated it, or the righteousness God gives, by which the believer lives? His own account of the discovery comes in the preface to his collected Latin works in 1545, thirty years after the fact; the dating of the breakthrough is one of the standing questions of Luther scholarship, with proposals ranging across most of the 1510s, and the narrative is generally treated as a later reconstruction rather than a diary.","What followed: justification by faith alone, faith being trust rather than assent; a righteousness imputed rather than infused, so that the believer is at once righteous and a sinner; the sharp distinction of law and gospel; and the bondage of the will, argued in 1525 against Erasmus, whom Luther thanked for having gone for the throat instead of quibbling over indulgences. Against this Trent taught in 1547 that justification is both the remission of sins and a real interior renewal, that it is wholly gratuitous in its beginning, that faith is its root but that a faith without charity does not save, and that the justified may merit an increase of grace. Both sides condemned Pelagianism; Trent's first canon on justification anathematises the claim that anyone can be justified by his own works without divine grace. The disagreement was about the nature of the justifying act, about merit thereafter, about assurance, and about whether justification and sanctification are one movement or two.","There was one near-miss. At the Colloquy of Regensburg in 1541, Contarini and Gropper for the Catholics and Melanchthon and Bucer for the Protestants agreed an article on a double righteousness — imputed and inherent, the first the ground of acceptance and the second its inseparable fruit. It was repudiated by Luther as fudge and by Rome as concession, and both were probably right about the politics and wrong about the theology. The confessions then hardened: the Augsburg Confession, the Formula of Concord resolving the Osiandrian question of whether Christ's indwelling divinity is our righteousness, Article XI of the Thirty-Nine Articles, the Westminster Confession, the canons of Dort, and the Wesleyan insistence that assurance and holiness belong to the doctrine and not to its footnotes.","Two twentieth-century movements changed the terms. The New Perspective on Paul — beginning with Krister Stendahl's essay of 1963 on the introspective conscience of the West and E. P. Sanders's Paul and Palestinian Judaism of 1977, developed by Dunn and Wright — argued that first-century Judaism was not a religion of earning salvation, that Luther had read a medieval anxiety into Paul, and that works of the law means the boundary markers separating Jew from gentile. The case has been vigorously contested by Westerholm, Carson, Gathercole and others and the scholarship is genuinely divided. Meanwhile the Finnish school of Luther interpretation, reading his language of union with Christ, brought him unexpectedly close to the Eastern doctrine of deification."],outcome:"The Joint Declaration on the Doctrine of Justification, signed at Augsburg on Reformation Day 1999 by the Lutheran World Federation and the Catholic Church, states a common confession that by grace alone, in faith in Christ's saving work and not because of any merit on our part, we are accepted by God and receive the Holy Spirit, and finds that the condemnations of the sixteenth century do not apply to the teaching each partner presents there. The Methodists associated themselves in 2006, the Anglican Consultative Council in 2016, the Reformed in 2017. It has not produced a shared eucharist, its reception among Lutherans was contested from the start, and the Catholic Response of 1998 had to be answered by an Annex before signature. And the whole quarrel remains, from the Eastern vantage, a Western one: Orthodox theology reads salvation as participation in the divine life rather than as a verdict in a court, and has watched five hundred years of argument about a metaphor it does not put at the centre.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Luther, preface to the Latin writings (1545) — Luther's Works vol. 34; WA 54",note:"The retrospective account of the discovery, written thirty years after, which is the text the entry treats as reconstruction rather than record."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Luther, The Freedom of a Christian (1520) and The Bondage of the Will (1525) — Luther's Works vols 31 and 33",note:"The doctrine stated positively and then defended against Erasmus, whom Luther thanked for going to the throat of the matter instead of quibbling over indulgences."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Council of Trent, Session VI, Decree and Canons on Justification — Tanner, Decrees of the Ecumenical Councils, vol. 2; numbered in Denzinger",note:"What Trent actually taught, first canon included, which is what the modern agreements had to establish was not what the Reformers denied."},{kind:"lexicon",ref:"Robert Kolb and Timothy J. Wengert (eds.), The Book of Concord (Minneapolis, 2000)",note:"Augsburg IV and Formula of Concord III in a modern critical translation — the Lutheran confession and the Osiandrian question it had to settle."},{kind:"primary",ref:"The Thirty-Nine Articles, XI; The Westminster Confession of Faith, XI",note:"The Anglican and Reformed statements, which differ from the Lutheran over assurance and the place of the law and so make 'the Protestant doctrine' a plural."},{kind:"study",ref:"Anthony N. S. Lane, Regensburg Article 5 on Justification (London, 2019)",note:"The 1541 double-righteousness article with the analysis of why Luther called it fudge and Rome called it concession."},{kind:"study",ref:"Alister E. McGrath, Iustitia Dei: A History of the Christian Doctrine of Justification (Cambridge)",note:"The standard history of the doctrine's whole development, and the place to check what in Trent is medieval and what in Luther is new."},{kind:"study",ref:"Krister Stendahl, 'The Apostle Paul and the Introspective Conscience of the West,' Harvard Theological Review 56 (1963); E. P. Sanders, Paul and Palestinian Judaism (Philadelphia, 1977)",note:"The two works that began the New Perspective and the case that Luther read a later anxiety back into Paul."},{kind:"study",ref:"Stephen Westerholm, Perspectives Old and New on Paul: The 'Lutheran' Paul and His Critics (Grand Rapids, 2004)",note:"The most careful reply from the other side, and the evidence that the exegetical question is genuinely open rather than settled by 1977."},{kind:"study",ref:"Carl E. Braaten and Robert W. Jenson (eds.), Union with Christ: The New Finnish Interpretation of Luther (Grand Rapids, 1998)",note:"The Finnish reading of Luther's language of union, and the bridge it proposes to the Eastern doctrine of deification."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Joint Declaration on the Doctrine of Justification (Lutheran World Federation and the Catholic Church, 1999), with the Official Common Statement and the Annex",note:"The common confession and its stated limits; the Annex exists because the Catholic Response of 1998 judged the first text insufficient, and that history is part of the document."},{kind:"study",ref:"Karl Lehmann and Wolfhart Pannenberg (eds.), The Condemnations of the Reformation Era: Do They Still Divide? (Minneapolis, 1990)",note:"The German study that supplied the Joint Declaration's method — asking not whether the anathemas were right but whether they hit the partner's present teaching."},{kind:"primary",ref:"George Mastrantonis, Augsburg and Constantinople: The Correspondence between the Tübingen Theologians and Patriarch Jeremiah II (Brookline, 1982)",note:"The sixteenth-century exchange in which Orthodoxy first answered the Lutheran doctrine, and the earliest evidence for the entry's closing point about a family argument in another language."}]}]};export{e as default};
